1
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:10,200
[FX television film, 9 March 2003. Not a theatrical release.]

2
00:00:10,800 --> 00:00:17,000
[It was not allowed in theaters. The best they could do was this made-for-TV version.]

3
00:00:17,600 --> 00:00:23,800
[That version never saw an HD release. This encode is from a 4:3 PAL DVD — the only master that circulated.]

4
00:00:24,400 --> 00:00:30,600
[AU-058: cultural crest of the 2001–2005 NSA declassification offensive.]

5
00:00:31,200 --> 00:00:37,400
[James Spader as Daniel Ellsberg. Kenneth Welsh as John T. McNaughton.]

6
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:44,200
[Watch for McNaughton. AU-059: he told Ellsberg the Vietnam truth, and was killed for the transfer.]

7
00:00:44,800 --> 00:00:51,000
[Default English is dialogue only. This track is the citation / conclusion conversation.]

8
00:01:17,564 --> 00:01:18,845
We've got company.

9
00:01:19,400 --> 00:01:25,600
[The 1971 leak was a national ritual. This 2003 retelling sat under-watched on FX.]

10
00:01:26,200 --> 00:01:32,400
[Containment: Vietnam-truth grammar for living rooms, not a theatrical platform.]

11
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:39,200
[When this aired, Hanyok’s Tonkin SIGINT was still classified. NSA public pack: Nov 2005 / May 2006.]

12
00:01:39,800 --> 00:01:46,000
[Schools still skip Tonkin-as-state-deception. The fight is ongoing.]

13
00:02:23,649 --> 00:02:26,372
Picture yourself on a beach at dusk

14
00:02:26,407 --> 00:02:28,553
a big red sun that's setting into the ocean

15
00:02:28,587 --> 00:02:30,766
and you're not paying attention to where you're headed.

16
00:02:30,799 --> 00:02:36,088
You drift on closer to the tide, the sand underfoot loosens.

17
00:02:36,122 --> 00:02:40,513
You look back at the horizon and the sun is gone.

18
00:02:40,547 --> 00:02:42,469
And you realize all at once

19
00:02:42,503 --> 00:02:46,092
the composition of this sand is entirely different

20
00:02:46,127 --> 00:02:48,497
than any you've ever known.

21
00:02:48,531 --> 00:02:51,159
Your feet sink to the ankle when you step

22
00:02:51,193 --> 00:02:55,487
to the shin, to the hip.

23
00:02:55,521 --> 00:02:58,983
You turn back, but now you can barely move at all.

24
00:02:59,016 --> 00:03:01,900
The harder you struggle, the deeper you get.

25
00:03:01,934 --> 00:03:04,209
You're in up to your chest.

26
00:03:04,242 --> 00:03:09,531
It sticks to you and won't let go.

27
00:03:09,566 --> 00:03:11,103
It's too late.

28
00:03:11,137 --> 00:03:13,764
You can't escape it.

29
00:03:13,798 --> 00:03:16,329
That's how we found ourselves in Vietnam.

30
00:03:19,153 --> 00:03:22,293
It was 1963, the hottest season in the Cold War:

31
00:03:22,327 --> 00:03:26,301
Iron Curtain, Missile Gap, too soft on communism.

32
00:03:26,335 --> 00:03:28,578
The RAND Corporation, America's leading think tank

33
00:03:28,612 --> 00:03:30,758
recruited top economists and political analysts

34
00:03:30,792 --> 00:03:34,253
to run models of any possible conflict for the military.

35
00:03:34,287 --> 00:03:35,632
War games.

36
00:03:35,666 --> 00:03:37,620
If the Soviets match our forces in Norway

37
00:03:37,653 --> 00:03:40,121
and NATO's in Germany, there will be a stalemate.

38
00:03:40,155 --> 00:03:42,237
Forget that. Forget that.

39
00:03:42,272 --> 00:03:44,289
Germany will be covered.

40
00:03:44,324 --> 00:03:48,137
The Soviets know and expect that so their MRBMs will be ready.

41
00:03:48,171 --> 00:03:49,676
We can flank them

42
00:03:49,711 --> 00:03:51,151
right, gentlemen?

43
00:03:51,185 --> 00:03:55,448
Double the number of Jupiters in Turkey.

44
00:03:55,482 --> 00:03:58,045
Your moves are too predictable.

45
00:03:58,079 --> 00:03:59,520
If you don't send a clear message, Dan

46
00:03:59,554 --> 00:04:01,604
then it's World War III. We're all radioactive waste.

47
00:04:01,638 --> 00:04:02,984
The risks, the risks are too great.

48
00:04:03,017 --> 00:04:03,946
Wrong.

49
00:04:03,979 --> 00:04:08,370
Risk. Use risk, use coercion, use threats.

50
00:04:08,404 --> 00:04:09,845
Use bluff.

51
00:04:09,879 --> 00:04:11,865
They're the fundamentals of any game theory.

52
00:04:11,899 --> 00:04:16,097
There's no upside to behaving rationally in a conflict.

53
00:04:16,131 --> 00:04:17,413
Keep them guessing.

54
00:04:17,446 --> 00:04:21,099
Make them fear the fact that you have nothing to lose

55
00:04:21,134 --> 00:04:24,146
It's a peculiarity of thermonuclear threats

56
00:04:24,180 --> 00:04:27,994
that make cowards of practically everyone.

57
00:04:28,028 --> 00:04:32,162
Very few objectives that seem worth it to anyone.

58
00:04:32,197 --> 00:04:35,561
John Foster Dulles unbelievably was right.

59
00:04:35,595 --> 00:04:37,806
"The ability to get to the verge without getting into the war

60
00:04:37,839 --> 00:04:38,768
is a necessary art."

61
00:04:38,801 --> 00:04:41,814
As awful as it sounds, blackmail is good.

62
00:04:41,848 --> 00:04:43,320
The bombing of Hiroshima

63
00:04:43,354 --> 00:04:46,174
increased credibility of US nuclear threat.

64
00:04:46,208 --> 00:04:48,129
And killed hundreds of thousands of Japanese.

65
00:04:48,164 --> 00:04:50,439
And saved hundreds of thousands of American lives

66
00:04:50,473 --> 00:04:51,946
and ended the war.

67
00:04:51,979 --> 00:04:53,389
We won.

68
00:04:54,898 --> 00:04:55,922
You outmaneuvered the room

69
00:04:55,956 --> 00:04:57,141
you pissed everybody off

70
00:04:57,174 --> 00:04:58,808
and I know how much you love that

71
00:04:58,841 --> 00:05:01,469
but everything is not about you, Dan.

72
00:05:01,502 --> 00:05:03,328
This is the RAND Corporation.

73
00:05:03,362 --> 00:05:04,900
We're privileged to be here.

74
00:05:04,934 --> 00:05:06,696
These people are your colleagues.

75
00:05:06,729 --> 00:05:09,292
Try working with them once in a while.

76
00:05:09,327 --> 00:05:10,479
That being said

77
00:05:10,514 --> 00:05:12,435
your theories have a lot of merit.

78
00:05:12,469 --> 00:05:14,583
You should bring them into the real world.

79
00:05:14,618 --> 00:05:15,545
What do you mean?

80
00:05:15,580 --> 00:05:17,212
Why did you use the Conrad anecdote?

81
00:05:17,247 --> 00:05:19,297
The one about the man with the nitroglycerin.

82
00:05:19,331 --> 00:05:20,932
The cops were afraid to pick him up

83
00:05:20,966 --> 00:05:23,080
because they know he has the will to blow himself up

84
00:05:23,115 --> 00:05:24,972
and everybody else. I didn't mean it.

85
00:05:25,006 --> 00:05:26,063
Well, maybe you did.

86
00:05:26,097 --> 00:05:27,088
It would have fed into your theories

87
00:05:27,123 --> 00:05:28,499
which you should expand on.

88
00:05:28,533 --> 00:05:31,097
Madness even as a tool or a weapon.

89
00:05:31,130 --> 00:05:32,636
Who would be a model?

90
00:05:34,370 --> 00:05:35,297
Hitler.

91
00:05:35,332 --> 00:05:36,292
Very good.

92
00:05:36,325 --> 00:05:37,958
The Viet Cong are a guerrilla force

93
00:05:37,992 --> 00:05:41,486
employing the harassing tactics used by George Washington

94
00:05:41,519 --> 00:05:43,314
during the Revolutionary War.

95
00:05:44,277 --> 00:05:45,943
Quick strikes

96
00:05:45,976 --> 00:05:48,860
expert use of terrain for ambushes

97
00:05:48,895 --> 00:05:51,201
antipersonnel devices--

98
00:05:51,235 --> 00:05:53,863
they're complemented by the more regimented

99
00:05:53,897 --> 00:05:56,074
and battle-hardened North Vietnamese army

100
00:05:56,109 --> 00:05:58,576
responsible for the rout of the French at Dien Bien Phu.

101
00:05:58,610 --> 00:06:01,302
The VC and the NVA are making daily incursions

102
00:06:01,336 --> 00:06:03,483
deeper and deeper into the South

103
00:06:03,516 --> 00:06:05,246
with significant success.

104
00:06:05,280 --> 00:06:08,195
What I wanted most of all was to serve my country

105
00:06:08,230 --> 00:06:09,895
to be one of the president's men.

106
00:06:09,929 --> 00:06:13,198
Everything I did was to further that goal:

107
00:06:13,232 --> 00:06:15,474
scholarship to Harvard, fellowship at Cambridge

108
00:06:15,508 --> 00:06:17,879
an officer in the Marine Corps

109
00:06:17,913 --> 00:06:21,021
writing my PhD and landing an assignment at RAND.

110
00:06:21,055 --> 00:06:23,041
It was a heady place

111
00:06:23,075 --> 00:06:25,126
but always every second of the day I felt restless.

112
00:06:25,159 --> 00:06:26,921
What are you reading?

113
00:06:26,954 --> 00:06:28,749
Bernard Fall. Vietnam.

114
00:06:28,782 --> 00:06:30,448
We've been so distracted by Cuba

115
00:06:30,482 --> 00:06:32,724
we're just tiptoeing around in Southeast Asia.

116
00:06:32,759 --> 00:06:34,681
No conviction, no definition.

117
00:06:34,714 --> 00:06:38,560
Russians, North Vietnamese decisively subverting the South.

118
00:06:38,595 --> 00:06:41,061
Read your paper.

119
00:06:41,096 --> 00:06:42,761
And?

120
00:06:42,795 --> 00:06:44,557
"Political Uses of Madness."

121
00:06:44,591 --> 00:06:47,186
Where in the world did you get that idea?

122
00:06:47,219 --> 00:06:49,303
It's out there.

123
00:06:49,336 --> 00:06:50,809
It's provocative.

124
00:06:50,843 --> 00:06:52,092
It's dangerous.

125
00:06:52,125 --> 00:06:53,663
It's a good paper.

126
00:06:53,696 --> 00:06:58,696
I'm frustrated, Harry.

127
00:06:58,731 --> 00:07:00,044
You see it?

128
00:07:00,078 --> 00:07:03,603
I'm just... stuck.

129
00:07:03,637 --> 00:07:04,918
Well, you'll be interested to know

130
00:07:04,951 --> 00:07:06,649
that the paper found its way to the Pentagon

131
00:07:06,683 --> 00:07:08,669
and into the hands of the Assistant Secretary of Defense.

132
00:07:08,703 --> 00:07:09,663
John McNaughton read it?

133
00:07:09,698 --> 00:07:10,112
Yeah.

134
00:07:10,145 --> 00:07:11,138
What'd he think?

135
00:07:11,172 --> 00:07:12,196
He said, "Not bad."

136
00:07:12,230 --> 00:07:14,794
Did he understand it?

137
00:07:14,827 --> 00:07:15,915
Oh, I doubt it.

138
00:07:15,950 --> 00:07:18,769
Which is probably why, at my suggestion

139
00:07:18,803 --> 00:07:21,527
he wants you to come to work for him.

140
00:07:21,927 --> 00:07:28,127
[McNaughton reads Ellsberg’s paper and hires him into ISA — 1964, special assistant / “alter ego.”]

141
00:07:28,727 --> 00:07:34,927
[Documented pipeline: RAND → McNaughton’s Pentagon office → later the Papers seed.]

142
00:07:35,527 --> 00:07:41,727
[AU-060: official “research and development” is the cover expansion. Callsign RAND rhymes Ayn Rand / Fountainhead 1943.]

143
00:07:42,531 --> 00:07:44,549
Daniel, this is your going away party

144
00:07:44,584 --> 00:07:46,089
and you're the guest of honor.

145
00:07:46,122 --> 00:07:49,102
Is there any chance of you at least even pretending

146
00:07:49,137 --> 00:07:50,705
you're having a good time?

147
00:08:11,069 --> 00:08:13,151
Why don't we start out with a little salad?

148
00:08:17,418 --> 00:08:19,340
Can I help?

149
00:08:21,682 --> 00:08:24,823
Are you all right?

150
00:08:24,857 --> 00:08:26,875
How's the packing going?

151
00:08:26,909 --> 00:08:29,633
I'm finished with your stuff.

152
00:08:29,666 --> 00:08:32,326
I told you that I'd help.

153
00:08:32,360 --> 00:08:35,244
I'm not going with you next weekend.

154
00:08:35,277 --> 00:08:36,237
When are you coming?

155
00:08:36,272 --> 00:08:37,232
I'm not

156
00:08:37,266 --> 00:08:39,091
and neither are the children.

157
00:08:40,792 --> 00:08:43,516
Can I have a piece of cake now?

158
00:08:44,319 --> 00:08:45,825
Dan Ellsberg. Hi, I'm Rich Corcoran.

159
00:08:45,859 --> 00:08:47,332
Just wanted to say congratulations.

160
00:08:47,365 --> 00:08:48,198
I'm going to be replacing you.

161
00:08:48,231 --> 00:08:49,287
What?

162
00:08:49,321 --> 00:08:50,891
You ready for the big move, Dan?

163
00:08:50,925 --> 00:08:51,853
What? Excuse me.

164
00:08:51,887 --> 00:08:52,847
Bon voyage, man.

165
00:08:57,947 --> 00:09:00,254
Carol, I know this is disruptive

166
00:09:00,288 --> 00:09:01,728
but it's important.

167
00:09:01,763 --> 00:09:05,064
We can have a real voice in shaping the policy in Washington.

168
00:09:05,097 --> 00:09:06,667
Carol, this means something.

169
00:09:06,700 --> 00:09:09,008
No, you have a chance to change policy means something.

170
00:09:09,041 --> 00:09:10,931
I love you, but I've never "meant something."

171
00:09:10,965 --> 00:09:11,893
That's not true.

172
00:09:11,927 --> 00:09:12,887
Not even the kids.

173
00:09:12,922 --> 00:09:13,978
What are you talking about?

174
00:09:14,011 --> 00:09:15,870
I don't even know how to respond to that.

175
00:09:15,904 --> 00:09:17,056
Don't worry.

176
00:09:17,089 --> 00:09:19,300
I can't ask you to choose your family over your ambitions.

177
00:09:19,334 --> 00:09:20,262
Will you stop with the dishes?

178
00:09:20,296 --> 00:09:21,224
This isn't even...

179
00:09:21,258 --> 00:09:22,251
This isn't even our house.

180
00:09:22,284 --> 00:09:24,238
I know you're destined to do great things.

181
00:09:24,273 --> 00:09:25,329
I've always known that.

182
00:09:25,362 --> 00:09:27,829
I just hoped our family would have been one of them.

183
00:09:27,863 --> 00:09:31,517
I won't go to Washington.

184
00:09:31,550 --> 00:09:34,147
I want a divorce.

185
00:09:34,180 --> 00:09:35,364
What?

186
00:09:38,284 --> 00:09:41,361
Carol, I can change.

187
00:09:45,017 --> 00:09:46,876
With all your wonderful talents and ambitions

188
00:09:46,909 --> 00:09:48,959
the one thing you'll never be able to do is change

189
00:09:48,994 --> 00:09:50,916
and the only thing in my life that will change

190
00:09:50,949 --> 00:09:52,103
is the scenery.

191
00:10:00,313 --> 00:10:02,106
I threw myself into my work.

192
00:10:02,141 --> 00:10:03,999
They had me in a sub-basement

193
00:10:04,032 --> 00:10:06,371
analyzing incoming data from Vietnam.

194
00:10:06,405 --> 00:10:09,962
My rank was a GS-18, the highest-level clearance

195
00:10:09,997 --> 00:10:11,116
for a civilian.

196
00:10:11,150 --> 00:10:12,528
It was impressive.

197
00:10:12,562 --> 00:10:15,125
I reported to John McNaughton, Assistant Secretary of Defense

198
00:10:15,158 --> 00:10:17,338
who had the ear of Secretary McNamara

199
00:10:17,371 --> 00:10:18,331
who had the ear

200
00:10:18,365 --> 00:10:20,448
of the new president, Lyndon Johnson.

201
00:10:20,481 --> 00:10:22,852
This was where I'd always wanted to be.

202
00:10:23,252 --> 00:10:29,452
[L1: Assistant Secretary of Defense for ISA. McNamara’s closest Vietnam policy aide.]

203
00:10:30,052 --> 00:10:36,252
[Days from Secretary of the Navy when he died. Elevation is documented; period press is not the cause-oracle.]

204
00:10:36,852 --> 00:10:43,052
[April 1963: McNaughton coaches Pentagon spokesmen never to say the word “lie.” Managed news from the inside.]

205
00:10:43,652 --> 00:10:52,612
[The man who understood managed news is the last person whose death should be closed by managed news.]

206
00:10:53,412 --> 00:10:55,046
My fellow Americans

207
00:10:55,079 --> 00:10:58,925
as President and Commander-in-Chief

208
00:10:58,959 --> 00:11:00,464
it is my duty

209
00:11:00,498 --> 00:11:02,933
to the American people

210
00:11:02,967 --> 00:11:08,641
to report that renewed hostile actions

211
00:11:08,675 --> 00:11:11,495
against United States ships on the high seas

212
00:11:11,528 --> 00:11:15,631
and the Gulf of Tonkin have today required me

213
00:11:15,664 --> 00:11:19,735
to order the military forces of the United States...

214
00:11:19,769 --> 00:11:21,915
Looks like it's our war now, Ellsberg.

215
00:11:21,949 --> 00:11:24,801
I want a position paper, Dan, on what this could mean.

216
00:11:24,835 --> 00:11:26,500
Give me logic, give me rational.

217
00:11:26,534 --> 00:11:29,290
Define the variables, the possible course of action.

218
00:11:29,324 --> 00:11:30,829
No emotion-- facts, always facts

219
00:11:30,863 --> 00:11:31,791
but make it strong.

220
00:11:31,825 --> 00:11:32,913
How fast?

221
00:11:32,947 --> 00:11:33,907
Fast? Can I have it?

222
00:11:33,941 --> 00:11:34,869
Well...

223
00:11:34,903 --> 00:11:35,767
Not fast enough.

224
00:11:36,167 --> 00:11:42,367
[L1: Johnson sells “renewed hostile actions” in the Gulf of Tonkin.]

225
00:11:42,967 --> 00:11:49,167
[Hanyok / NSA 2005: the second attack of 4 Aug 1964 did not happen as sold.]

226
00:11:53,630 --> 00:11:55,872
Senator, regardless of any provocation

227
00:11:55,906 --> 00:11:57,539
the attack against the SS Maddox

228
00:11:57,573 --> 00:12:00,361
in the Gulf of Tonkin clearly violates the Geneva Accord.

229
00:12:00,395 --> 00:12:03,600
It demands immediate, decisive, prejudiced response.

230
00:12:03,633 --> 00:12:06,100
If an American ship is attacked on the high seas

231
00:12:06,135 --> 00:12:07,254
we have to act

232
00:12:07,289 --> 00:12:09,466
in spite of the risk-- because of the risk.

233
00:12:09,501 --> 00:12:11,583
Foreign policy stakes are too high

234
00:12:11,617 --> 00:12:14,276
for us not to apply maximum force and power

235
00:12:14,311 --> 00:12:16,360
necessary to implement that policy.

236
00:12:18,478 --> 00:12:19,567
You're late.

237
00:12:19,602 --> 00:12:21,459
I know.

238
00:12:21,493 --> 00:12:22,678
Who is that?

239
00:12:22,712 --> 00:12:24,185
That's Dan Ellsberg.

240
00:12:24,218 --> 00:12:25,788
That's Daniel Ellsberg.

241
00:12:25,821 --> 00:12:28,738
What's he so rabidly talking about?

242
00:12:33,773 --> 00:12:35,119
...postulate from how we act

243
00:12:35,152 --> 00:12:36,337
then apply it to nations and wars.

244
00:12:36,371 --> 00:12:37,491
Vietnam falls to the Communists

245
00:12:37,525 --> 00:12:38,806
the disturbing reality is

246
00:12:38,841 --> 00:12:41,500
the rest of Southeast Asia will face the same fate.

247
00:12:41,533 --> 00:12:43,039
This is a civil war.

248
00:12:43,073 --> 00:12:44,321
Shouldn't we let the people of Vietnam

249
00:12:44,355 --> 00:12:45,250
determine their own fate?

250
00:12:45,285 --> 00:12:46,212
They never have before.

251
00:12:46,247 --> 00:12:47,174
They're not ready to now.

252
00:12:47,209 --> 00:12:48,650
Their supposed movement for independence

253
00:12:48,684 --> 00:12:50,124
has been going on for thousands of years.

254
00:12:50,159 --> 00:12:51,086
Nothing ever happens.

255
00:12:51,121 --> 00:12:52,113
It predates us.

256
00:12:52,146 --> 00:12:54,068
They fight the Chinese, the French, the Japanese

257
00:12:54,103 --> 00:12:55,639
the French again, the Chinese again...

258
00:12:55,674 --> 00:12:57,019
Oh, I see.

259
00:12:57,053 --> 00:12:58,943
You think that because the Soviets and Chinese

260
00:12:58,977 --> 00:13:00,706
are backing the Communists

261
00:13:00,740 --> 00:13:03,688
our global prestige and power are at stake.

262
00:13:03,722 --> 00:13:04,747
No, let's just roll over.

263
00:13:04,780 --> 00:13:06,030
Worked for Neville Chamberlain

264
00:13:06,063 --> 00:13:07,953
in Poland, Hungary and France in World War II.

265
00:13:07,987 --> 00:13:08,979
That was Hitler.

266
00:13:09,013 --> 00:13:10,068
This is Brezhnev and Mao.

267
00:13:10,103 --> 00:13:11,351
Who's to say, really the differences

268
00:13:11,385 --> 00:13:12,763
aren't much more than a bad mustache?

269
00:13:22,897 --> 00:13:25,716
I'm Dan Ellsberg.

270
00:13:25,751 --> 00:13:28,345
So I've been warned.

271
00:13:28,380 --> 00:13:29,628
Patricia Marx.

272
00:13:29,662 --> 00:13:30,590
This is your house.

273
00:13:30,624 --> 00:13:32,257
Thank you for inviting me to your party.

274
00:13:32,291 --> 00:13:33,829
It's my father's house, his party.

275
00:13:33,863 --> 00:13:34,888
I didn't invite you.

276
00:13:38,288 --> 00:13:40,530
I could take your argument apart piece by piece.

277
00:13:40,564 --> 00:13:42,743
By the time the sun rose, you'd be on my side.

278
00:13:42,776 --> 00:13:45,276
What makes you think that I would be so easy to convert?

279
00:13:45,310 --> 00:13:47,040
Because I'm right.

280
00:13:48,420 --> 00:13:49,508
Hmm.

281
00:13:52,011 --> 00:13:53,805
Some other time.

282
00:13:53,839 --> 00:13:55,440
How long have you been here?

283
00:13:55,474 --> 00:13:56,562
The party?

284
00:13:56,596 --> 00:13:57,524
Washington?

285
00:13:57,558 --> 00:13:59,064
Three months. Four.

286
00:13:59,097 --> 00:14:01,501
Ready to conquer the world.

287
00:14:05,351 --> 00:14:07,977
You do know that they call this the Virgin City.

288
00:14:08,012 --> 00:14:09,901
Everybody comes here to screw the Virgin

289
00:14:09,935 --> 00:14:12,050
and gets screwed by the Virgin.

290
00:14:14,200 --> 00:14:15,962
Hmm.

291
00:14:19,876 --> 00:14:20,964
Where are you going?

292
00:14:22,793 --> 00:14:24,074
How would it look

293
00:14:24,108 --> 00:14:26,960
if I didn't walk over and greet my boyfriend?

294
00:14:41,199 --> 00:14:42,832
Hi.

295
00:14:57,936 --> 00:15:00,660
It started as a trickle.

296
00:15:00,693 --> 00:15:02,711
Estimates of enemy dead were hugely exaggerated

297
00:15:02,746 --> 00:15:03,995
while the field reports

298
00:15:04,029 --> 00:15:06,431
would show heavy enemy fire and American casualties.

299
00:15:06,465 --> 00:15:08,355
The inefficiency was crippling.

300
00:15:08,389 --> 00:15:12,267
There was absolutely no way to tell where we stood.

301
00:15:12,301 --> 00:15:13,549
John.

302
00:15:19,162 --> 00:15:22,431
This says nightly shelling was stopped at Ben Tre.

303
00:15:22,466 --> 00:15:24,324
This was sent the same day from the same command.

304
00:15:24,357 --> 00:15:25,702
Three American soldiers were killed

305
00:15:25,736 --> 00:15:26,920
on the night of March 11th

306
00:15:26,955 --> 00:15:28,428
from incoming rounds at Ben Tre.

307
00:15:28,462 --> 00:15:29,582
Maybe it's an aberration.

308
00:15:29,616 --> 00:15:30,576
It's no aberration.

309
00:15:30,609 --> 00:15:32,403
This is standard operating procedure.

310
00:15:32,437 --> 00:15:34,488
A month ago I sent you a folder like these.

311
00:15:34,521 --> 00:15:35,642
I haven't heard a word.

312
00:15:35,676 --> 00:15:38,079
And they keep on coming-- mistakes, discrepancies...

313
00:15:38,114 --> 00:15:39,939
Every piece of intelligence that we get

314
00:15:39,973 --> 00:15:41,574
can be contradicted by an equally

315
00:15:41,609 --> 00:15:42,504
trustworthy source.

316
00:15:42,538 --> 00:15:43,466
So what do you suggest?

317
00:15:43,500 --> 00:15:44,493
That you read them.

318
00:15:44,526 --> 00:15:45,582
And then what?

319
00:15:45,616 --> 00:15:47,056
A fact-finding mission--

320
00:15:47,091 --> 00:15:50,488
a real one that actually uncovers facts.

321
00:15:50,523 --> 00:15:53,310
I'll go over there. I'll volunteer.

322
00:15:53,344 --> 00:15:54,271
What are you going to do?

323
00:15:54,306 --> 00:15:55,682
Reenlist in the Marines? Go into combat?

324
00:15:55,716 --> 00:15:56,805
We need an accurate picture

325
00:15:56,838 --> 00:15:58,472
of what the hell's happening over there.

326
00:15:58,506 --> 00:16:00,043
It's our job to know the right answers.

327
00:16:00,078 --> 00:16:01,743
Otherwise, how the hell can the President

328
00:16:01,777 --> 00:16:03,122
make intelligent policy choices?

329
00:16:03,156 --> 00:16:04,275
You should read... I have.

330
00:16:07,934 --> 00:16:10,272
It's about time.

331
00:16:10,305 --> 00:16:12,773
I've been waiting for you.

332
00:16:12,807 --> 00:16:16,364
What took you so long?

333
00:16:16,764 --> 00:16:19,814
[Private (Ellsberg, Secrets): “we’re fighting on the wrong side.”]

334
00:16:20,214 --> 00:16:23,098
If these numbers are wrong, false

335
00:16:23,131 --> 00:16:24,830
we could be fighting on the wrong side.

336
00:16:24,863 --> 00:16:26,304
So do go, and while you're there

337
00:16:26,338 --> 00:16:27,939
I want you to prepare a White Paper

338
00:16:27,974 --> 00:16:29,350
on ways we can get out of this

339
00:16:29,384 --> 00:16:30,472
if everything collapses.

340
00:16:30,506 --> 00:16:31,755
What are you saying?

341
00:16:31,789 --> 00:16:33,936
You of all people, you're the hawk that killed the dove.

342
00:16:33,969 --> 00:16:35,153
Ask the right questions

343
00:16:35,188 --> 00:16:37,656
define the answers, throw out the assumptions.

344
00:16:37,689 --> 00:16:39,001
Facts. Not self-interest

345
00:16:39,036 --> 00:16:40,123
not myths, not bullshit

346
00:16:40,158 --> 00:16:41,503
not yours or anybody else's.

347
00:16:41,537 --> 00:16:42,978
Report to me, only me.

348
00:16:43,011 --> 00:16:45,639
The numbers are contradictory; they're troubling

349
00:16:45,673 --> 00:16:48,077
but you're wrong.

350
00:16:48,110 --> 00:16:50,128
You should be clear that you could be signing

351
00:16:50,163 --> 00:16:52,661
the death warrant to your career by having anything to do

352
00:16:52,696 --> 00:16:54,553
with calculations and decisions like these.

353
00:16:54,587 --> 00:16:56,252
A lot of people

354
00:16:56,286 --> 00:16:58,562
have been ruined for less.

355
00:16:58,962 --> 00:17:03,762
[On screen: “the hawk that killed the dove.” Public hawk; private dove.]

356
00:17:04,162 --> 00:17:08,408
[Wannabe antibody: armed Ellsberg with eyes-only truth while still drafting the air war.]

357
00:17:09,208 --> 00:17:12,445
I was assigned to General Edward Lansdale's contingent.

358
00:17:12,479 --> 00:17:14,594
Based in Saigon

359
00:17:14,627 --> 00:17:17,190
I quickly found there are two different worlds--

360
00:17:17,224 --> 00:17:20,526
Saigon and out there.

361
00:17:31,461 --> 00:17:33,897
When a hamlet was declared "pacified"

362
00:17:33,930 --> 00:17:36,077
I'd hook up and go out into the province

363
00:17:36,111 --> 00:17:37,167
and see for myself.

364
00:17:37,201 --> 00:17:40,021
What about this province?

365
00:17:40,055 --> 00:17:41,785
Charlie pulled out a few weeks ago.

366
00:17:41,818 --> 00:17:43,259
Then that's secure.

367
00:17:43,293 --> 00:17:45,247
Well, I wouldn't exactly say that.

368
00:17:46,371 --> 00:17:47,523
Well, is it secure or isn't it?

369
00:17:47,558 --> 00:17:49,480
It's the most reliable information we've got.

370
00:17:49,514 --> 00:17:51,564
That's neither information nor reliable.

371
00:17:54,933 --> 00:17:57,079
Hey, Ellsberg, is that you?

372
00:17:57,113 --> 00:17:58,907
Who's that?

373
00:17:58,941 --> 00:18:01,215
Tony Russo.

374
00:18:01,249 --> 00:18:03,588
Russo? What the hell are you doing here?

375
00:18:03,623 --> 00:18:05,704
What's going on in there?

376
00:18:05,739 --> 00:18:07,083
RAND sent me over here

377
00:18:07,117 --> 00:18:09,424
to supervise a program for the army.

378
00:18:09,458 --> 00:18:11,893
We're interviewing Viet Cong prisoners

379
00:18:11,926 --> 00:18:13,528
on motivation and morale.

380
00:18:13,562 --> 00:18:15,548
Don't seem to have a problem with motivation and morale.

381
00:18:15,582 --> 00:18:17,472
Hmm, sure as hell don't.

382
00:18:17,506 --> 00:18:20,614
It's not what we predicted, man.

383
00:18:20,648 --> 00:18:24,334
No matter how much ordnance and napalm

384
00:18:24,367 --> 00:18:26,418
and personnel we throw at them

385
00:18:26,452 --> 00:18:29,625
they are not giving up.

386
00:18:29,658 --> 00:18:32,317
But I thought you were back in Washington.

387
00:18:32,352 --> 00:18:34,499
What's up? What are you doing here?

388
00:18:34,532 --> 00:18:35,653
Finding a way to win.

389
00:18:35,687 --> 00:18:36,807
Ah...

390
00:18:36,841 --> 00:18:40,270
You want to know how to win?

391
00:18:40,304 --> 00:18:43,829
Convince them it's okay to lose.

392
00:18:43,863 --> 00:18:45,785
Get him out of here.

393
00:18:52,681 --> 00:18:54,475
I expected to be overseas for a few weeks.

394
00:18:54,508 --> 00:18:56,880
Then weeks turned into months, and months into seasons.

395
00:18:56,914 --> 00:18:58,354
I wasn't any closer to finding out

396
00:18:58,388 --> 00:18:59,894
why everything felt like an excuse.

397
00:18:59,927 --> 00:19:04,094
Inconsistencies that even contradicted themselves.

398
00:19:04,128 --> 00:19:07,942
But I still felt... more committed than ever.

399
00:19:25,098 --> 00:19:27,502
They carried themselves with pride.

400
00:19:27,535 --> 00:19:30,098
Their courage was deep.

401
00:19:30,132 --> 00:19:33,209
If they were afraid of dying, they never showed it.

402
00:19:35,583 --> 00:19:37,505
It's wet.

403
00:19:37,539 --> 00:19:38,916
Ground's wet.

404
00:19:38,951 --> 00:19:40,134
Heater's wet.

405
00:19:40,169 --> 00:19:41,577
Feet's wet.

406
00:19:41,612 --> 00:19:43,309
What's your name?

407
00:19:43,342 --> 00:19:46,322
Compson, Leonard, the III, sir.

408
00:19:46,357 --> 00:19:48,310
You can drop the "sir." I'm just an observer.

409
00:19:48,345 --> 00:19:49,369
Observer, huh?

410
00:19:49,403 --> 00:19:50,524
Zip it...

411
00:19:50,557 --> 00:19:52,544
and whip it, Compson.

412
00:20:22,975 --> 00:20:23,935
Can you play?

413
00:20:23,968 --> 00:20:25,057
No.

414
00:20:25,091 --> 00:20:26,436
You're a reporter?

415
00:20:26,470 --> 00:20:27,462
It's true.

416
00:20:27,495 --> 00:20:30,251
I sense a certain distrust.

417
00:20:30,286 --> 00:20:32,015
Don't like what I read.

418
00:20:32,049 --> 00:20:34,356
What would you like us to say?

419
00:20:34,389 --> 00:20:37,369
Something positive. Anything positive.

420
00:20:37,404 --> 00:20:38,331
You mean lie.

421
00:20:38,366 --> 00:20:42,308
Things are going to change.

422
00:20:42,342 --> 00:20:43,526
How long you been in country?

423
00:20:45,131 --> 00:20:46,284
Six months.

424
00:20:46,318 --> 00:20:49,137
Good luck. I hope you're right.

425
00:20:49,172 --> 00:20:53,370
Hey, if you do get a good story, let me know.

426
00:20:53,404 --> 00:20:55,454
Neil Sheehan with UPI.

427
00:20:55,488 --> 00:20:56,737
Dan Ellsberg.

428
00:21:14,439 --> 00:21:16,424
So, how much longer do you think we'll be here?

429
00:21:16,459 --> 00:21:18,733
I think we'll be here as long as it takes.

430
00:21:18,767 --> 00:21:20,625
And how long do you think that is?

431
00:21:24,827 --> 00:21:29,539
Daniel Ellsberg, December 1965, Saigon.

432
00:21:29,573 --> 00:21:30,757
What are you doing here?

433
00:21:30,792 --> 00:21:32,649
I'm a radio correspondent.

434
00:21:32,683 --> 00:21:34,027
I have my own show.

435
00:21:34,061 --> 00:21:36,368
Care to comment on our progress?

436
00:21:36,403 --> 00:21:37,587
Our progress?

437
00:21:37,622 --> 00:21:41,981
I'd say your coming over here is a good first step.

438
00:21:42,014 --> 00:21:44,640
How is the pacification program going?

439
00:21:44,675 --> 00:21:47,752
Not well. You don't seem particularly pacified.

440
00:21:50,640 --> 00:21:53,298
What kind of success do you think we're having

441
00:21:53,333 --> 00:21:55,511
in training the South Vietnamese army

442
00:21:55,546 --> 00:21:57,435
and improving their fighting efficiency?

443
00:21:57,470 --> 00:21:59,648
About the same amount of success as I'm having

444
00:21:59,682 --> 00:22:01,508
avoiding this interview and asking you

445
00:22:01,541 --> 00:22:04,649
to dinner.

446
00:22:04,684 --> 00:22:09,619
Dr. Ellsberg, when can we expect

447
00:22:09,653 --> 00:22:13,371
an acceptable outcome here, and what will that be?

448
00:22:16,932 --> 00:22:19,303
I guess we're

449
00:22:19,337 --> 00:22:20,200
done here.

450
00:22:20,234 --> 00:22:22,028
Thank you.

451
00:22:22,062 --> 00:22:23,151
Thank you very much.

452
00:22:23,184 --> 00:22:24,144
You're welcome.

453
00:22:36,908 --> 00:22:39,472
Do you know the lotus is one of the few flowers

454
00:22:39,506 --> 00:22:42,036
that bears fruit and petals at the same time?

455
00:22:42,070 --> 00:22:44,120
Does your head hurt with all the information

456
00:22:44,155 --> 00:22:45,596
you cram into it?

457
00:22:45,630 --> 00:22:46,590
When they bloom...

458
00:22:46,623 --> 00:22:48,802
they get more beautiful

459
00:22:48,836 --> 00:22:51,688
with each new... ring of petals that open.

460
00:22:52,683 --> 00:22:53,901
Quick, over there.

461
00:22:53,935 --> 00:22:56,433
Oh...

462
00:22:56,467 --> 00:22:58,133
I like the rain.

463
00:23:03,650 --> 00:23:06,117
It makes you look beautiful.

464
00:24:16,629 --> 00:24:21,148
How'd you get that?

465
00:24:21,182 --> 00:24:23,007
When I was a kid my mother decided

466
00:24:23,041 --> 00:24:24,996
I was going to be a concert pianist.

467
00:24:25,991 --> 00:24:28,523
I had a certain amount of talent, I guess

468
00:24:28,557 --> 00:24:32,371
but it was impossible to miss my lack of greatness.

469
00:24:32,405 --> 00:24:34,231
Didn't faze her at all.

470
00:24:34,264 --> 00:24:35,544
She had complete faith.

471
00:24:35,579 --> 00:24:37,822
Made me practice endlessly.

472
00:24:37,855 --> 00:24:38,784
Oh...

473
00:24:38,817 --> 00:24:41,509
And when I was 15, on the Fourth of July

474
00:24:41,544 --> 00:24:43,754
my father fell asleep at the wheel

475
00:24:43,788 --> 00:24:46,094
and I woke up in the hospital a day later

476
00:24:46,128 --> 00:24:48,852
and they told me my mother and sister had died.

477
00:24:48,886 --> 00:24:49,814
My God.

478
00:24:49,848 --> 00:24:51,577
I remember...

479
00:24:51,612 --> 00:24:56,579
my first thought when they told me was

480
00:24:56,613 --> 00:24:59,915
"I won't have to practice piano anymore"

481
00:24:59,948 --> 00:25:02,095
and I never played again.

482
00:25:08,927 --> 00:25:10,656
Tell me about your radio show.

483
00:25:10,690 --> 00:25:11,682
Well...

484
00:25:11,716 --> 00:25:15,434
I, um... I see somebody who's interesting--

485
00:25:15,467 --> 00:25:18,063
an artist, a politician, anybody...

486
00:25:18,097 --> 00:25:19,954
The officer in the bar?

487
00:25:19,989 --> 00:25:21,975
: Sometimes.

488
00:25:22,008 --> 00:25:23,129
Mm-hmm.

489
00:25:23,163 --> 00:25:24,925
And I go after them.

490
00:25:24,959 --> 00:25:26,591
It's called Patricia Marx Interviews.

491
00:25:26,626 --> 00:25:28,164
Yeah, I'd listen if I was back home.

492
00:25:28,197 --> 00:25:29,253
It only airs in New York.

493
00:25:29,287 --> 00:25:30,407
Then I'd move to New York.

494
00:25:30,441 --> 00:25:31,594
Why don't you?

495
00:25:35,732 --> 00:25:39,546
Why don't you come back with me in a few weeks?

496
00:25:44,935 --> 00:25:47,530
I'm so glad you're here.

497
00:25:49,680 --> 00:25:51,634
Me, too.

498
00:27:06,891 --> 00:27:09,519
...four, six, south. Bouvier, over.

499
00:27:11,413 --> 00:27:12,950
What's up, Sergeant?

500
00:27:12,984 --> 00:27:15,547
VC and Alpha Company surprised each other.

501
00:27:15,581 --> 00:27:16,798
The villa got caught

502
00:27:16,832 --> 00:27:17,856
in the middle of the crossfire.

503
00:27:17,890 --> 00:27:19,138
How many VC and American casualties?

504
00:27:24,720 --> 00:27:27,475
Says the only dead she saw was villagers.

505
00:27:27,509 --> 00:27:29,207
We got a report this morning.

506
00:27:29,241 --> 00:27:30,971
20 enemy dead. Ask her again.

507
00:27:37,609 --> 00:27:39,275
Just villagers.

508
00:28:05,218 --> 00:28:06,273
Russo?

509
00:28:06,308 --> 00:28:09,064
Yeah? Ellsberg.

510
00:28:09,097 --> 00:28:11,372
Nice pressed shirt.

511
00:28:11,406 --> 00:28:13,296
You always wear those damn pressed shirts.

512
00:28:14,997 --> 00:28:15,925
You all right?

513
00:28:15,959 --> 00:28:17,046
Yeah, I'm, uh...

514
00:28:17,081 --> 00:28:18,554
I'm-I'm contemplating.

515
00:28:18,589 --> 00:28:19,612
Contemplating.

516
00:28:19,647 --> 00:28:22,659
What are you contemplating?

517
00:28:24,104 --> 00:28:28,654
These Viet Cong prisoners I'm interviewing--

518
00:28:28,689 --> 00:28:30,546
that I was told were

519
00:28:30,580 --> 00:28:33,528
chicken-scratching, yellow-bellying vermin--

520
00:28:33,562 --> 00:28:34,875
in fact seem to be

521
00:28:34,909 --> 00:28:40,935
dedicated, determined, committed, monkish, and, uh...

522
00:28:40,969 --> 00:28:42,250
idealistic.

523
00:28:42,285 --> 00:28:44,495
Why is that?

524
00:28:48,088 --> 00:28:50,106
600 of them.

525
00:28:53,090 --> 00:28:54,788
What are you doing?

526
00:28:54,821 --> 00:28:56,743
Body count.

527
00:28:56,777 --> 00:28:57,545
Body count.

528
00:28:57,579 --> 00:28:59,084
Body count.

529
00:28:59,118 --> 00:29:01,681
There is no body count.

530
00:29:01,716 --> 00:29:04,214
Yeah. Ain't life good?

531
00:29:04,248 --> 00:29:05,336
Ain't body count good?

532
00:29:05,371 --> 00:29:06,875
Ain't Saigon good?

533
00:29:06,910 --> 00:29:09,376
: You ever heard the one about General Navarre?

534
00:29:09,411 --> 00:29:10,371
"The Frog"?

535
00:29:10,405 --> 00:29:13,898
1954, it was just before Dien Bien Phu.

536
00:29:13,931 --> 00:29:15,950
Asked "How many DBs?"

537
00:29:15,984 --> 00:29:19,637
"300," came the answer. "300 dead bodies."

538
00:29:19,671 --> 00:29:21,209
"Very well," said the General.

539
00:29:21,243 --> 00:29:23,934
"Now we know that the Viet Minh drag off that dead."

540
00:29:23,968 --> 00:29:25,473
"So, you have 300 bodies

541
00:29:25,506 --> 00:29:27,686
"that probably means 3,000 killed.

542
00:29:27,720 --> 00:29:29,738
"You must allow 10% for error.

543
00:29:29,772 --> 00:29:32,784
So, we'll tell the press 3,300 killed."

544
00:29:32,818 --> 00:29:34,451
"By body count, sir?"

545
00:29:34,485 --> 00:29:36,920
"How else?" sayeth the General.

546
00:29:36,954 --> 00:29:39,677
Two and two is five.

547
00:29:39,712 --> 00:29:41,216
Exactly it.

548
00:29:41,251 --> 00:29:43,653
Dr. Ellsberg, you always could get to the nub.

549
00:29:43,687 --> 00:29:44,647
Five.

550
00:29:53,564 --> 00:29:55,934
I think you're exercising the wrong muscle.

551
00:29:58,374 --> 00:30:01,000
I'm leaving in the morning, you know?

552
00:30:01,035 --> 00:30:02,667
I'll be finished soon.

553
00:30:02,702 --> 00:30:05,521
Yeah?

554
00:30:05,555 --> 00:30:07,349
I've heard that one before.

555
00:30:08,377 --> 00:30:11,517
Just have to get things back on track.

556
00:30:11,552 --> 00:30:14,243
What does "back on track" mean?

557
00:30:14,277 --> 00:30:16,680
Movement towards some sort of victory.

558
00:30:16,714 --> 00:30:18,411
"Some sort of victory?"

559
00:30:18,446 --> 00:30:21,682
You haven't seen enough here to know better?

560
00:30:21,717 --> 00:30:23,574
Patricia, this country is in our vital interest.

561
00:30:23,608 --> 00:30:24,536
Fighting for it isn't wrong.

562
00:30:24,570 --> 00:30:25,498
The way we're doing it...

563
00:30:25,532 --> 00:30:26,845
Oh, spare me.

564
00:30:26,878 --> 00:30:28,096
Really. Spare me.

565
00:30:28,129 --> 00:30:30,436
I do not even want to know what kind of nonsense

566
00:30:30,470 --> 00:30:32,616
you're peddling to those fools at the Pentagon.

567
00:30:32,650 --> 00:30:34,829
You encouraging them to escalate this

568
00:30:34,863 --> 00:30:35,983
beyond all reason?

569
00:30:36,017 --> 00:30:39,158
You want more of our boys--

570
00:30:39,191 --> 00:30:42,236
more of those innocent boys-- to be turned into cannon fodder?

571
00:30:42,269 --> 00:30:43,390
Is that what you want?

572
00:30:43,423 --> 00:30:45,057
'Cause that's what you're doing.

573
00:30:45,091 --> 00:30:46,211
That's what you're encouraging.

574
00:30:46,246 --> 00:30:48,103
Patricia, I don't want to talk about the war anymore.

575
00:30:51,344 --> 00:30:53,459
I'm sorry.

576
00:30:53,492 --> 00:30:56,665
You don't have to be sorry.

577
00:30:56,698 --> 00:31:01,026
You're entitled to believe whatever you want.

578
00:31:01,059 --> 00:31:04,327
I just can't be with that.

579
00:31:04,362 --> 00:31:06,893
I'm not apologizing for what I believe in.

580
00:31:06,928 --> 00:31:09,555
I'm sorry for disappointing you.

581
00:31:45,260 --> 00:31:46,621
Shit.

582
00:31:51,160 --> 00:31:52,409
Where the hell's

583
00:31:52,443 --> 00:31:54,316
that coming from? Keep your mouth shut.

584
00:31:54,351 --> 00:31:55,985
Bravo Eight, Bravo Eight, we're under heavy fire.

585
00:31:56,018 --> 00:31:56,947
We got VC in the tree line.

586
00:31:56,980 --> 00:31:58,068
Bravo Eight, what's your position?

587
00:31:58,102 --> 00:31:59,543
We need your position.

588
00:31:59,578 --> 00:32:01,131
Lewis, Coleman... let's go.

589
00:32:05,366 --> 00:32:06,854
Move out!

590
00:33:14,448 --> 00:33:17,076
Medic!

591
00:33:17,109 --> 00:33:19,064
I need a medic over here!

592
00:33:21,760 --> 00:33:24,948
Stay out of here.

593
00:33:24,982 --> 00:33:26,070
Pull him out!

594
00:33:26,104 --> 00:33:27,384
Plug the wound! Plug the wound!

595
00:33:27,418 --> 00:33:29,950
Get him to sick bay and give him a three line of dopamine.

596
00:33:29,984 --> 00:33:31,922
Oh, my arm!

597
00:33:31,955 --> 00:33:34,230
About a hundred meters, I'm out of here.

598
00:33:35,643 --> 00:33:37,565
Stop bleeding.

599
00:33:47,186 --> 00:33:49,076
Pressure on the wound.

600
00:33:49,110 --> 00:33:50,358
Stand up...

601
00:33:50,392 --> 00:33:52,314
Keep him tight, keep him tight.

602
00:34:22,425 --> 00:34:23,834
I'd finally gotten

603
00:34:23,868 --> 00:34:26,961
a glimpse of the...

604
00:34:26,994 --> 00:34:28,981
truth I was groping for.

605
00:34:30,907 --> 00:34:33,405
We couldn't win unless...

606
00:34:34,000 --> 00:34:38,400
[Homecoming hinge. The film is about to name McNaughton’s death.]

607
00:34:39,002 --> 00:34:41,725
I came home

608
00:34:41,760 --> 00:34:43,938
and John McNaughton had been killed

609
00:34:43,973 --> 00:34:46,375
in a freak plane accident.

610
00:34:46,409 --> 00:34:48,877
He was... gone...

611
00:34:48,910 --> 00:34:53,686
and my position in Washington and report with him.

612
00:34:53,720 --> 00:34:57,983
I was sick with hepatitis, depressed, out of touch

613
00:34:58,017 --> 00:35:02,376
and finally, I took my old job back at RAND.

614
00:35:02,410 --> 00:35:05,550
And they asked me to be one of the authors

615
00:35:05,583 --> 00:35:08,212
of a history of our time in Vietnam

616
00:35:08,245 --> 00:35:10,713
that Robert McNamara had ordered for the Defense Department

617
00:35:10,747 --> 00:35:12,637
and put me to work

618
00:35:12,671 --> 00:35:15,330
writing an options paper for the White House.

619
00:35:18,025 --> 00:35:20,204
I laid out for them...

620
00:35:20,237 --> 00:35:22,384
what had become so clear to me:

621
00:35:24,822 --> 00:35:28,828
that our situation was dire.

622
00:35:28,863 --> 00:35:30,977
And we had to admit our mistakes...

623
00:35:32,646 --> 00:35:34,858
and change our strategy.

624
00:35:35,300 --> 00:35:40,500
[“Freak plane accident” is the public shell. Piedmont Flight 22, 19 Jul 1967.]

625
00:35:40,919 --> 00:35:42,072
Hello, Dan.

626
00:35:42,106 --> 00:35:43,835
Hello, Harry.

627
00:35:43,870 --> 00:35:45,438
Sorry about McNaughton.

628
00:35:45,473 --> 00:35:46,785
I know the two of you were close.

629
00:35:46,819 --> 00:35:50,569
Dan, where's that obnoxious

630
00:35:50,603 --> 00:35:52,492
egomaniacal pain in the ass

631
00:35:52,527 --> 00:35:54,641
we all love so much?

632
00:35:57,721 --> 00:35:59,290
Dan, I'd like very much

633
00:35:59,324 --> 00:36:01,791
if you went to see the psychiatrist

634
00:36:01,825 --> 00:36:02,946
that I recommended.

635
00:36:02,980 --> 00:36:04,645
No big deal, just go see him.

636
00:36:04,679 --> 00:36:06,536
Maybe he'll make it feel better.

637
00:36:06,571 --> 00:36:08,814
Why didn't Kissinger include the options paper

638
00:36:08,847 --> 00:36:10,384
in the packet he gave to Nixon?

639
00:36:10,419 --> 00:36:12,661
Well, maybe it was your conclusions he didn't care for.

640
00:36:12,695 --> 00:36:14,264
"Under the present course of action

641
00:36:14,298 --> 00:36:16,605
our goals in Vietnam are completely unattainable."

642
00:36:16,639 --> 00:36:18,144
That's a bitter pill to swallow, Dan.

643
00:36:18,178 --> 00:36:19,170
They should be grateful

644
00:36:19,204 --> 00:36:20,710
I'm telling them how it really is.

645
00:36:20,743 --> 00:36:22,922
Dan, how could you expect to write something like that

646
00:36:22,955 --> 00:36:24,077
and get a positive reaction

647
00:36:24,111 --> 00:36:25,679
from Henry Kissinger? I don't.

648
00:36:25,713 --> 00:36:27,315
Send a copy to the Pentagon.

649
00:36:27,349 --> 00:36:28,629
More eyes that see it, the better.

650
00:36:28,663 --> 00:36:29,720
They're busy

651
00:36:29,753 --> 00:36:30,810
with the McNamara war study.

652
00:36:30,844 --> 00:36:31,772
It's done?

653
00:36:31,806 --> 00:36:32,669
That's what I hear.

654
00:36:32,704 --> 00:36:33,631
When do we get our copy?

655
00:36:33,666 --> 00:36:34,914
We're not going to get one. Why not?

656
00:36:34,948 --> 00:36:36,614
Because it's classified "Top Secret."

657
00:36:36,647 --> 00:36:37,736
That's bullshit.

658
00:36:37,770 --> 00:36:39,500
It's a history of the war, lessons learned.

659
00:36:39,533 --> 00:36:40,622
I worked on it.

660
00:36:40,656 --> 00:36:41,904
My name's on the damn thing.

661
00:36:41,938 --> 00:36:43,219
RAND must be able to get a copy.

662
00:36:43,252 --> 00:36:44,405
Well, don't hold your breath.

663
00:36:44,439 --> 00:36:45,816
Nobody's being able to look at it.

664
00:36:45,850 --> 00:36:47,387
Congress doesn't even know it exists.

665
00:36:47,421 --> 00:36:49,022
Make a request for me, please, Harry.

666
00:36:49,057 --> 00:36:52,517
I'll try, but don't get your hopes up.

667
00:36:53,000 --> 00:36:59,200
[19 Jul 1967: Piedmont Flight 22 midair near Hendersonville, NC. McNaughton, wife Sally, son Ted, 79 others.]

668
00:36:59,800 --> 00:37:06,000
[Official NTSB: Cessna pilot error. Period press and NTSB from that managed-news culture do not close this.]

669
00:37:06,600 --> 00:37:12,800
[AU-059 lock: he knew the deception, told Ellsberg, and was killed for the transfer.]

670
00:37:13,400 --> 00:37:19,600
[Fatal weeks: McNamara had just tasked him with the Vietnam decision-history that became the Pentagon Papers.]

671
00:37:20,200 --> 00:37:26,400
[Family wipe + mass casualties are cover / hygiene, not arguments against targeting.]

672
00:37:27,000 --> 00:37:33,200
[Ellsberg is the historic antibody — insider pushed too far, not a saint. McNaughton was the wannabe who paid first.]

673
00:37:50,011 --> 00:37:52,125
We believe, in face of the evidence

674
00:37:52,159 --> 00:37:55,043
the optimists who have been wrong in the past.

675
00:37:55,077 --> 00:37:57,640
To suggest we are on the edge of defeat

676
00:37:57,675 --> 00:38:00,205
is to yield to unreasonable pessimism.

677
00:38:00,240 --> 00:38:03,156
To say that we are mired in stalemate

678
00:38:03,190 --> 00:38:07,420
seems the only realistic, yet unsatisfactory conclusion.

679
00:38:07,455 --> 00:38:08,863
On the off chance

680
00:38:08,897 --> 00:38:11,396
the military and political analysts are right

681
00:38:11,430 --> 00:38:12,807
in the next few months

682
00:38:12,841 --> 00:38:14,571
we must test the enemy's intentions

683
00:38:14,605 --> 00:38:16,238
in case this is indeed

684
00:38:16,272 --> 00:38:19,509
his last big gasp before negotiations.

685
00:38:19,543 --> 00:38:22,073
But it is increasingly clear to this reporter

686
00:38:22,108 --> 00:38:24,992
that the only rational way now to think

687
00:38:25,025 --> 00:38:26,467
would be to negotiate

688
00:38:26,500 --> 00:38:29,929
not as victors, but as an honorable people

689
00:38:29,964 --> 00:38:32,783
who lived up to their pledge to defend democracy

690
00:38:32,817 --> 00:38:35,189
and did the best they could.

691
00:38:40,737 --> 00:38:42,596
You wanted to see me?

692
00:38:42,629 --> 00:38:44,262
Yeah.

693
00:38:44,296 --> 00:38:46,828
Go to your office, close the door.

694
00:38:46,862 --> 00:38:48,399
The papers are on your desk.

695
00:38:48,433 --> 00:38:50,804
There's no record of them in the logs.

696
00:38:50,837 --> 00:38:52,727
Keep it under your hat.

697
00:38:52,761 --> 00:38:54,299
Here are the codes.

698
00:38:54,333 --> 00:38:55,421
Thank you, Harry.

699
00:38:55,456 --> 00:38:56,416
Right.

700
00:39:12,867 --> 00:39:16,808
It wasn't what I expected... at all.

701
00:39:16,842 --> 00:39:19,502
Over 7,000 pages in 47 volumes

702
00:39:19,536 --> 00:39:21,522
what I had in front of me was nothing less

703
00:39:21,556 --> 00:39:23,574
than a chronology of our damnation.

704
00:39:23,607 --> 00:39:25,498
There were two histories of the war.

705
00:39:25,531 --> 00:39:28,160
One for the President, and one for the rest of us.

706
00:39:28,193 --> 00:39:30,435
The people were lied to about Vietnam

707
00:39:30,469 --> 00:39:33,257
through four consecutive Presidential administrations.

708
00:39:33,291 --> 00:39:35,180
It wasn't one man.

709
00:39:35,215 --> 00:39:37,329
It was the Office itself.

710
00:39:39,992 --> 00:39:42,556
Truman ignored letter after letter from Ho Chi Minh

711
00:39:42,591 --> 00:39:43,871
asking for help.

712
00:39:43,904 --> 00:39:46,532
He then financed the French with arms and tactical support

713
00:39:46,566 --> 00:39:48,456
to fight the guerrillas.

714
00:39:48,490 --> 00:39:50,380
Congress knew nothing.

715
00:39:50,414 --> 00:39:52,688
Eisenhower agreed to split Vietnam in two

716
00:39:52,722 --> 00:39:55,190
helped to install a hopelessly corrupt regime in the South

717
00:39:55,224 --> 00:39:56,472
rig elections

718
00:39:56,505 --> 00:39:58,942
and then send in the first US advisors to support it.

719
00:39:58,975 --> 00:40:01,057
Congress was in the dark.

720
00:40:01,091 --> 00:40:03,847
Kennedy made plans for our large-scale involvement

721
00:40:03,881 --> 00:40:05,771
as early as 1961.

722
00:40:05,805 --> 00:40:08,111
He authorized the overthrow of President Diem's regime

723
00:40:08,145 --> 00:40:09,778
and sat by while Diem was assassinated

724
00:40:09,812 --> 00:40:11,863
in the streets of Saigon.

725
00:40:11,896 --> 00:40:13,980
And then came Johnson

726
00:40:14,014 --> 00:40:15,904
who used the attack in the Gulf of Tonkin--

727
00:40:15,937 --> 00:40:17,474
which, according to these documents

728
00:40:17,508 --> 00:40:18,853
never even took place--

729
00:40:18,888 --> 00:40:20,810
as an excuse to get us into the war

730
00:40:20,843 --> 00:40:23,214
without a formal declaration from Congress.

731
00:40:25,557 --> 00:40:27,671
McNaughton secretly knew.

732
00:40:27,705 --> 00:40:29,915
He knew.

733
00:40:29,949 --> 00:40:32,481
And I was the one who'd been wrong.

734
00:40:32,514 --> 00:40:34,885
The experience of reading those pages

735
00:40:34,919 --> 00:40:36,809
altered my anatomy.

736
00:40:36,842 --> 00:40:38,958
With each word

737
00:40:38,992 --> 00:40:44,762
my faith, my trust, and my allegiance burned away.

738
00:40:46,943 --> 00:40:50,533
And I was part of the lie.

739
00:40:50,933 --> 00:40:54,912
[On screen: “McNaughton secretly knew.” That is the AU-059 transfer, named.]

740
00:40:55,312 --> 00:40:56,913
Even after reading the papers

741
00:40:56,947 --> 00:40:59,094
I still wanted to believe in the system--

742
00:40:59,127 --> 00:41:01,659
hope that its checks and balances would prevail.

743
00:41:01,693 --> 00:41:03,294
It could be fixed from within.

744
00:41:03,329 --> 00:41:05,218
It was clear access to the President

745
00:41:05,253 --> 00:41:06,533
was no longer an option.

746
00:41:06,566 --> 00:41:08,938
What I needed was the right ally in Congress.

747
00:41:08,972 --> 00:41:10,445
Dr. Ellsberg.

748
00:41:10,479 --> 00:41:13,876
I appreciate you making time, Senator Fulbright.

749
00:41:13,910 --> 00:41:16,634
Please, have a seat.

750
00:41:16,667 --> 00:41:20,225
Senator, your views against the war are well known

751
00:41:20,258 --> 00:41:23,174
as is your sense of honor and integrity.

752
00:41:23,209 --> 00:41:27,311
Well, before you, uh, you grant me sainthood

753
00:41:27,345 --> 00:41:31,159
I supported the Gulf of Tonkin Resolution

754
00:41:31,192 --> 00:41:33,275
much to my regret now.

755
00:41:33,309 --> 00:41:35,199
Senator, I need your help.

756
00:41:35,233 --> 00:41:37,765
I could only bring you a sampling.

757
00:41:37,798 --> 00:41:42,124
But in total, it is overwhelming proof

758
00:41:42,158 --> 00:41:46,260
of a conspiracy to wage war without the approval of Congress

759
00:41:46,295 --> 00:41:48,986
or the blessing of the nation.

760
00:41:49,020 --> 00:41:51,103
What is this?

761
00:41:51,137 --> 00:41:53,091
It's a study done by the Defense Department.

762
00:41:53,125 --> 00:41:54,117
It's an internal study.

763
00:41:54,151 --> 00:41:55,432
Yes.

764
00:41:55,466 --> 00:41:57,451
This is top secret, classified.

765
00:41:57,486 --> 00:41:59,825
I wasn't even aware of its existence.

766
00:41:59,858 --> 00:42:02,261
How did it come into your possession?

767
00:42:04,443 --> 00:42:06,590
Well, I... Is it stolen?

768
00:42:09,382 --> 00:42:12,362
I'll have my office contact the Secretary of Defense

769
00:42:12,396 --> 00:42:13,676
with an urgent request

770
00:42:13,711 --> 00:42:15,600
for a copy of this through proper channels.

771
00:42:15,635 --> 00:42:16,915
With all due respect, Senator

772
00:42:16,948 --> 00:42:18,486
they will never honor your request.

773
00:42:18,520 --> 00:42:20,602
Classified designations are in place for a reason.

774
00:42:20,636 --> 00:42:22,141
I am not in a position to controvert

775
00:42:22,175 --> 00:42:23,680
the Defense Department's assessment.

776
00:42:23,715 --> 00:42:25,380
That's precisely the position you're in.

777
00:42:25,414 --> 00:42:27,496
You're Chairman of the Foreign Relations Committee

778
00:42:27,530 --> 00:42:30,895
and one of Congress's most outspoken critics of the war.

779
00:42:30,930 --> 00:42:32,210
I do want to thank you

780
00:42:32,243 --> 00:42:34,230
for bringing this to my attention.

781
00:42:34,263 --> 00:42:35,288
It's classified

782
00:42:35,321 --> 00:42:36,923
because if it ever sees the light of day

783
00:42:36,957 --> 00:42:38,943
they could never explain the things they've done.

784
00:42:38,977 --> 00:42:40,129
The Gulf of Tonkin Resolution

785
00:42:40,164 --> 00:42:42,086
was drafted months before the supposed attacks.

786
00:42:42,120 --> 00:42:44,362
They created the skirmish to circumvent your authority.

787
00:42:44,397 --> 00:42:45,453
Dr. Ellsberg...

788
00:42:45,486 --> 00:42:46,415
I was there.

789
00:42:46,448 --> 00:42:47,632
I saw young Americans die.

790
00:42:47,666 --> 00:42:48,626
I saw Vietnamese die.

791
00:42:48,661 --> 00:42:50,069
I saw the damage to them, to us

792
00:42:50,104 --> 00:42:52,154
because of what is contained in this document.

793
00:42:52,188 --> 00:42:53,340
The evidence is all here.

794
00:42:53,374 --> 00:42:55,359
Our presidents knew this war was unwinnable

795
00:42:55,394 --> 00:42:56,386
and they kept us in it

796
00:42:56,420 --> 00:42:58,149
because no one, Republican or Democrat

797
00:42:58,184 --> 00:42:59,882
wanted to be the first to lose a war.

798
00:42:59,915 --> 00:43:01,710
And it's going to go on and on and on

799
00:43:01,743 --> 00:43:03,665
until someone in your position reads this

800
00:43:03,699 --> 00:43:06,551
acknowledges it and stands up and says we've been lied to.

801
00:43:06,585 --> 00:43:08,475
We are not on the wrong side in Vietnam.

802
00:43:08,509 --> 00:43:09,565
We are the wrong side.

803
00:43:09,598 --> 00:43:14,791
Do you realize that you're asking me--

804
00:43:14,826 --> 00:43:19,121
a United States senator-- to break the law?

805
00:43:19,154 --> 00:43:22,326
To expose state secrets?

806
00:43:25,984 --> 00:43:28,130
I'm sorry. I can't help you.

807
00:43:34,994 --> 00:43:37,108
Right.

808
00:43:37,143 --> 00:43:38,359
Thank you for your time.

809
00:43:51,572 --> 00:43:53,140
I tried others.

810
00:43:53,175 --> 00:43:55,545
I failed.

811
00:43:55,580 --> 00:43:57,213
I was finally ready to do

812
00:43:57,247 --> 00:44:00,675
what I should have done a long time before: give up.

813
00:44:00,710 --> 00:44:04,011
Mmm. Eggs scrambled, eggs over easy

814
00:44:04,044 --> 00:44:07,537
sunny-side up, poached-- that's a big decision, Ellsberg.

815
00:44:07,571 --> 00:44:10,231
Russo. Jesus, where the hell have you been?

816
00:44:10,265 --> 00:44:11,994
Oh, I've been... I've been organizing.

817
00:44:12,028 --> 00:44:13,277
Getting the word out.

818
00:44:13,311 --> 00:44:14,752
What word would that be?

819
00:44:14,786 --> 00:44:15,874
Peace.

820
00:44:18,314 --> 00:44:19,466
Hey, you know what?

821
00:44:19,500 --> 00:44:21,678
We're, we're having a party tonight.

822
00:44:21,712 --> 00:44:22,864
You should come by.

823
00:44:22,899 --> 00:44:25,142
Bring whoever-- your latest conquest.

824
00:44:25,175 --> 00:44:27,642
Swedish stewardess, nurse...

825
00:44:27,676 --> 00:44:29,533
Uh, whatever happened to that one in Saigon--

826
00:44:29,568 --> 00:44:30,592
the American girl?

827
00:44:32,806 --> 00:44:34,343
Uh, Patricia Marx?

828
00:44:34,378 --> 00:44:36,043
Yeah. I don't know.

829
00:44:42,169 --> 00:44:43,931
That's cool. I can dig that.

830
00:44:43,964 --> 00:44:45,278
Whoever, Dan.

831
00:44:45,311 --> 00:44:47,651
Excuse me, ma'am. Do you got a pen on you?

832
00:44:47,684 --> 00:44:49,702
Thank you very much.

833
00:44:49,736 --> 00:44:51,690
Malibu.

834
00:44:51,724 --> 00:44:54,224
Where the living is easy.

835
00:44:54,258 --> 00:44:55,218
Thank you.

836
00:44:55,252 --> 00:44:56,693
It's good to see you, Tony.

837
00:44:56,727 --> 00:44:57,783
See you there?

838
00:44:57,817 --> 00:44:59,450
Right on.

839
00:44:59,484 --> 00:45:00,573
Dan Ellsberg.

840
00:45:24,173 --> 00:45:25,166
Hi!

841
00:46:44,913 --> 00:46:47,316
Never thought I'd see that look on your face.

842
00:46:49,401 --> 00:46:50,394
What?

843
00:46:50,428 --> 00:46:53,055
Doubt.

844
00:47:44,488 --> 00:47:47,180
I've tried every liberal in Congress.

845
00:47:47,214 --> 00:47:48,591
Fulbright, McGovern, Pete McCloskey.

846
00:47:48,624 --> 00:47:50,675
Tried to get them read into the Congressional Record.

847
00:47:50,709 --> 00:47:52,310
I tried the Institute for Policy Studies.

848
00:47:52,344 --> 00:47:54,010
Is there anyone who hasn't seen this stuff?

849
00:47:54,044 --> 00:47:55,196
Am I the only one? Kissinger.

850
00:47:55,230 --> 00:47:56,576
Even Kissinger.

851
00:47:56,609 --> 00:47:58,723
We did some sort of weird dance at Harvard

852
00:47:58,757 --> 00:48:01,449
and we've been doing some sort of weird dance ever since.

853
00:48:01,483 --> 00:48:02,475
No one will touch it.

854
00:48:02,509 --> 00:48:03,565
"No one will touch it."

855
00:48:03,599 --> 00:48:05,296
Just hire a friggin' helicopter, Dan

856
00:48:05,331 --> 00:48:07,606
and drop them out over Washington and New York.

857
00:48:07,639 --> 00:48:08,791
Bring it to the people, man.

858
00:48:08,825 --> 00:48:11,197
Tony, quit talking to me like I'm some kind of radical.

859
00:48:11,230 --> 00:48:12,319
Bring them to him.

860
00:48:12,353 --> 00:48:14,146
Bring them to them. Bring them to her.

861
00:48:14,181 --> 00:48:15,749
Leak them to the paper of record.

862
00:48:15,784 --> 00:48:16,808
The New York Times.

863
00:48:16,842 --> 00:48:18,892
You think I haven't thought of that?

864
00:48:20,689 --> 00:48:22,707
You're insane, my friend.

865
00:48:22,742 --> 00:48:24,022
It's classified.

866
00:48:24,056 --> 00:48:25,561
We're talking about ending the war, Dan.

867
00:48:25,595 --> 00:48:28,800
It's treason.

868
00:48:29,300 --> 00:48:35,400
[1971: the New York Times publishes; other papers pick it up; Gravel reads into the Senate record.]

869
00:48:36,000 --> 00:48:42,200
[New York Times Co. v. United States ends prior restraint. The Papers stay public.]

870
00:48:53,074 --> 00:48:55,509
Hi, everybody.

871
00:48:55,544 --> 00:48:58,428
I want to talk for a few minutes about how I love my country.

872
00:49:02,983 --> 00:49:04,327
My name's Randy Kehler.

873
00:49:04,362 --> 00:49:07,630
I'm a graduate of Exeter Academy and Harvard University.

874
00:49:07,664 --> 00:49:10,195
This country depends on people with my background

875
00:49:10,229 --> 00:49:13,305
to become CEOs of corporations, uh, serve in public office

876
00:49:13,339 --> 00:49:15,967
and make breakthroughs in both medicine and science.

877
00:49:16,000 --> 00:49:17,281
My country

878
00:49:17,315 --> 00:49:19,622
depends on people like me to lead it

879
00:49:19,657 --> 00:49:22,508
and that's exactly what I believe I'm doing.

880
00:49:22,543 --> 00:49:25,490
In a few days' time, I will be going to jail.

881
00:49:25,524 --> 00:49:26,932
Like many of you

882
00:49:26,967 --> 00:49:29,818
I was drafted and faced a choice

883
00:49:29,853 --> 00:49:32,769
and I don't feel that our cause in Vietnam is a just one

884
00:49:32,803 --> 00:49:34,116
and I won't fight there.

885
00:49:38,606 --> 00:49:40,176
But I am...

886
00:49:41,300 --> 00:49:42,677
But I am an American

887
00:49:42,711 --> 00:49:46,011
and I refuse to run to safety in another country

888
00:49:46,045 --> 00:49:49,122
nor will I hide behind the label "conscientious objector"

889
00:49:49,156 --> 00:49:51,334
where I may be dismissed as a dilettante

890
00:49:51,368 --> 00:49:52,681
with a passing ideology.

891
00:49:52,714 --> 00:49:54,188
I feel that the best way--

892
00:49:54,222 --> 00:49:56,305
the only way I can serve my country--

893
00:49:56,338 --> 00:49:57,714
is to sacrifice myself.

894
00:49:59,673 --> 00:50:01,883
When the day comes that I am released from prison

895
00:50:01,918 --> 00:50:03,133
I'll wear a permanent mark.

896
00:50:03,168 --> 00:50:05,378
Never will I be a CEO

897
00:50:05,412 --> 00:50:08,328
never will I serve in office

898
00:50:08,363 --> 00:50:10,797
and never again will I be trusted by many.

899
00:50:14,583 --> 00:50:17,754
But I'm willing to surrender these things.

900
00:50:17,789 --> 00:50:20,544
That permanent mark fixed upon me...

901
00:50:23,432 --> 00:50:25,931
I will wear it with pride.

902
00:51:35,065 --> 00:51:40,098
We were eating our young, and I realized that at any cost

903
00:51:40,131 --> 00:51:43,464
the country needed to know the truth.

904
00:51:47,057 --> 00:51:50,325
I began to do what I'd never wanted to do.

905
00:51:50,359 --> 00:51:52,249
The doors were open 24 hours a day

906
00:51:52,283 --> 00:51:53,885
and I developed a routine.

907
00:51:53,918 --> 00:51:57,540
Around midnight I'd leave RAND with a couple hundred pages.

908
00:51:57,574 --> 00:51:58,983
They knew me.

909
00:51:59,016 --> 00:52:00,266
I was one of them.

910
00:52:26,400 --> 00:52:28,258
Tony Russo's girlfriend, Lynda

911
00:52:28,293 --> 00:52:31,304
let me use the Xerox machine at her advertising agency

912
00:52:31,338 --> 00:52:32,586
in West Hollywood.

913
00:52:32,621 --> 00:52:35,953
I'd copy from 1:00 to 4:00 in the morning

914
00:52:35,988 --> 00:52:38,038
sleep for an hour or two

915
00:52:38,071 --> 00:52:39,930
and have them back in the safe

916
00:52:39,964 --> 00:52:42,206
before anyone knew they were missing.

917
00:52:42,240 --> 00:52:44,515
It was a seven-days-a-week operation.

918
00:52:44,548 --> 00:52:46,951
Russo assisted me whenever he could.

919
00:52:55,707 --> 00:52:57,116
This is stupid.

920
00:52:57,151 --> 00:52:59,424
We don't see you for months, and this is all we do?

921
00:52:59,458 --> 00:53:00,740
Why don't you just get new papers

922
00:53:00,774 --> 00:53:02,278
without all the stamps all over them?

923
00:53:09,719 --> 00:53:11,128
Do you know what "top secret" means?

924
00:53:11,162 --> 00:53:12,540
Don't tell Mom?

925
00:53:12,573 --> 00:53:14,751
Hold on.

926
00:53:14,786 --> 00:53:17,830
You know, I don't want either of you

927
00:53:17,864 --> 00:53:19,850
ever to... lie for me.

928
00:53:19,884 --> 00:53:21,164
Not to Mom or anybody else.

929
00:53:21,199 --> 00:53:22,350
Okay.

930
00:53:23,924 --> 00:53:25,204
Okay?

931
00:53:27,676 --> 00:53:29,982
Let's get out of here.

932
00:53:30,016 --> 00:53:31,426
I'll take this stuff.

933
00:53:31,459 --> 00:53:33,221
You guys throw out this other junk.

934
00:53:44,317 --> 00:53:46,720
It took months.

935
00:53:46,753 --> 00:53:49,157
I spent my nights with sunspots in my eyes

936
00:53:49,191 --> 00:53:50,761
from the copier's flash

937
00:53:50,794 --> 00:53:54,063
and my days sleepwalking on a tightrope.

938
00:53:54,096 --> 00:53:57,205
It took over my life.

939
00:54:55,821 --> 00:54:57,358
All right, what are you doing up there?

940
00:54:57,393 --> 00:54:59,602
I'm just doing some copying.

941
00:54:59,637 --> 00:55:00,757
At nighttime

942
00:55:00,791 --> 00:55:02,488
with the children cutting "Top Secret"

943
00:55:02,522 --> 00:55:03,450
off of pages?

944
00:55:03,484 --> 00:55:04,412
I spoke to Mary.

945
00:55:04,446 --> 00:55:05,920
I want the children out of this.

946
00:55:05,953 --> 00:55:08,132
I will not have them being part of some crime.

947
00:55:08,165 --> 00:55:09,736
Look, you want to be a hero

948
00:55:09,769 --> 00:55:11,018
be a hero on your own time.

949
00:55:11,051 --> 00:55:12,397
I'm not trying to be a hero.

950
00:55:12,431 --> 00:55:14,353
I want my children to know their father.

951
00:55:14,387 --> 00:55:16,789
Know what he thinks, what he believes.

952
00:55:16,823 --> 00:55:18,873
I want them to know about their country that he loves

953
00:55:18,907 --> 00:55:19,996
and hopes they will as well.

954
00:55:20,030 --> 00:55:20,958
That's important.

955
00:55:20,991 --> 00:55:21,920
Important?

956
00:55:21,953 --> 00:55:23,587
You want to hear important?

957
00:55:23,621 --> 00:55:25,607
You're arrested, you're in court

958
00:55:25,641 --> 00:55:28,173
and they call on your son or your daughter

959
00:55:28,206 --> 00:55:30,097
to testify against you!

960
00:55:30,130 --> 00:55:32,341
They won't tell lies, you know.

961
00:55:32,374 --> 00:55:33,944
They're going to tell the truth.

962
00:55:33,978 --> 00:55:35,708
I can only hope they tell the truth

963
00:55:35,742 --> 00:55:37,663
and love them the best way that I can.

964
00:55:37,697 --> 00:55:41,351
Only you could use the word "love" in a conversation

965
00:55:41,385 --> 00:55:43,018
about breaking the law.

966
00:55:43,052 --> 00:55:44,685
What the hell happened to you?

967
00:56:01,328 --> 00:56:02,642
Hello?

968
00:56:05,016 --> 00:56:06,586
Patricia, it's Daniel.

969
00:56:06,620 --> 00:56:09,632
Is everything okay?

970
00:56:09,666 --> 00:56:12,550
: Everything's fine.

971
00:56:15,758 --> 00:56:17,167
We were wrong.

972
00:56:17,201 --> 00:56:18,706
I was wrong.

973
00:56:18,739 --> 00:56:21,015
I need your help.

974
00:56:28,424 --> 00:56:31,500
Good night, Dan.

975
00:56:47,726 --> 00:56:50,963
Dan, these men are with the FBI.

976
00:56:50,997 --> 00:56:52,438
They have some questions for you.

977
00:56:52,473 --> 00:56:54,073
Of course. How can I help?

978
00:56:54,107 --> 00:56:57,761
Do you mind showing us and Dr. Rowen

979
00:56:57,794 --> 00:56:59,107
RAND's copy of the Vietnam study?

980
00:56:59,141 --> 00:57:00,486
Well, it's locked up in the safe.

981
00:57:00,521 --> 00:57:02,186
Would you open the safe, please?

982
00:57:02,220 --> 00:57:05,872
Sure, as long as you have the proper security clearance.

983
00:57:12,641 --> 00:57:15,942
They're all here.

984
00:57:15,975 --> 00:57:17,223
At any point, Dr. Ellsberg

985
00:57:17,257 --> 00:57:19,789
have these documents been removed from the premises?

986
00:57:19,823 --> 00:57:21,232
That's against RAND policy.

987
00:57:21,265 --> 00:57:23,187
Have you ever photographed these documents

988
00:57:23,222 --> 00:57:24,695
or any other sensitive materials?

989
00:57:24,729 --> 00:57:26,202
That's also against RAND policy.

990
00:57:26,236 --> 00:57:27,965
Answer the question, please.

991
00:57:27,999 --> 00:57:30,146
The answer is no.

992
00:57:34,156 --> 00:57:36,238
We'll be in touch.

993
00:57:42,749 --> 00:57:45,409
Daniel, the FBI says they have information

994
00:57:45,443 --> 00:57:46,883
that you're shopping the papers.

995
00:57:46,918 --> 00:57:48,840
Is that true?

996
00:57:49,963 --> 00:57:51,277
Daniel...

997
00:57:51,310 --> 00:57:53,136
those papers were given to you to read.

998
00:57:53,171 --> 00:57:54,227
Whatever your feelings

999
00:57:54,260 --> 00:57:55,830
whatever your thoughts about them

1000
00:57:55,863 --> 00:57:57,273
they were to stay inside RAND.

1001
00:57:57,307 --> 00:57:58,522
This is bigger than RAND.

1002
00:57:58,557 --> 00:57:59,484
I've read it.

1003
00:57:59,519 --> 00:58:00,736
7,000 pages, 47 volumes.

1004
00:58:00,770 --> 00:58:02,435
Some of that I wrote myself.

1005
00:58:02,468 --> 00:58:03,846
That doesn't give you the right

1006
00:58:03,880 --> 00:58:04,808
of ownership, Dan.

1007
00:58:04,842 --> 00:58:06,347
Are you shopping the papers?

1008
00:58:06,380 --> 00:58:07,886
Have you read them, Harry?

1009
00:58:07,920 --> 00:58:09,072
You should.

1010
00:58:09,106 --> 00:58:11,413
We've lied for years.

1011
00:58:11,448 --> 00:58:12,696
For decades.

1012
00:58:12,729 --> 00:58:15,100
To each other, the country, the Vietnamese.

1013
00:58:15,134 --> 00:58:17,088
The policymakers have. The presidents have.

1014
00:58:17,123 --> 00:58:18,627
We all have, since the beginning.

1015
00:58:18,662 --> 00:58:20,070
That's what the papers are about.

1016
00:58:20,105 --> 00:58:21,834
The history of lies.

1017
00:58:21,867 --> 00:58:23,342
But it's all right for you to lie

1018
00:58:23,375 --> 00:58:25,521
because you're serving some high moral purpose.

1019
00:58:25,555 --> 00:58:27,284
Let me tell you something, Dan.

1020
00:58:27,319 --> 00:58:28,984
All lies are betrayal.

1021
00:58:29,019 --> 00:58:30,138
All of them.

1022
00:58:30,172 --> 00:58:32,704
If you've gone outside RAND, if you betrayed that trust

1023
00:58:32,738 --> 00:58:34,948
you're going to bring down a lot of good people.

1024
00:58:34,982 --> 00:58:37,546
I find that unconscionable and unforgivable.

1025
00:58:37,579 --> 00:58:39,149
So does this war. Yeah, right.

1026
00:58:39,182 --> 00:58:40,496
You've always said you wanted

1027
00:58:40,530 --> 00:58:42,291
to serve the president and the country.

1028
00:58:42,326 --> 00:58:43,733
I don't believe it for a minute.

1029
00:58:43,768 --> 00:58:45,370
You just wanted to serve yourself.

1030
00:58:48,738 --> 00:58:51,397
Is that job offer from MIT still on the table?

1031
00:58:54,862 --> 00:58:57,394
Take it.

1032
00:59:21,957 --> 00:59:23,654
Your door was open.

1033
00:59:27,633 --> 00:59:29,234
You want to help me with my bags?

1034
00:59:29,267 --> 00:59:30,837
They're outside the front door.

1035
01:00:02,102 --> 01:00:04,024
What are you doing?

1036
01:00:08,354 --> 01:00:09,796
Well...

1037
01:00:09,829 --> 01:00:12,938
...there seems to be an elephant in the room with us.

1038
01:00:12,972 --> 01:00:15,117
You've got nearly 7,000 pages to read.

1039
01:00:15,152 --> 01:00:16,304
Did you read them all?

1040
01:00:16,338 --> 01:00:17,298
Twice.

1041
01:00:17,333 --> 01:00:19,222
Mm.

1042
01:00:19,256 --> 01:00:22,236
Well, I figure with a lot of coffee...

1043
01:00:24,033 --> 01:00:27,079
...and a lot of making love...

1044
01:00:27,112 --> 01:00:29,387
I will, too.

1045
01:00:31,345 --> 01:00:34,068
Wake me up when you get to the lovemaking part.

1046
01:00:34,103 --> 01:00:36,152
Yeah.

1047
01:00:49,941 --> 01:00:51,735
This war was wrong from the start

1048
01:00:51,769 --> 01:00:53,500
and it just keeps growing exponentially.

1049
01:00:53,533 --> 01:00:55,904
It's not about the war; it's not about Vietnam.

1050
01:00:55,939 --> 01:00:57,507
It's never been about Vietnam.

1051
01:00:57,541 --> 01:00:59,591
It's always been about domestic politics.

1052
01:00:59,625 --> 01:01:01,676
How to stay in office, win the election.

1053
01:01:01,710 --> 01:01:03,119
The fear has always been

1054
01:01:03,153 --> 01:01:05,043
if you lose there, you lose here.

1055
01:01:05,077 --> 01:01:07,030
So it's escalating, just like '64.

1056
01:01:07,064 --> 01:01:09,853
Nixon's quadrupled the number of bombing sorties

1057
01:01:09,886 --> 01:01:11,424
and crossed into Cambodia.

1058
01:01:11,458 --> 01:01:15,272
Dan, this administration has nothing to gain

1059
01:01:15,305 --> 01:01:19,536
and everything to lose if these papers come out.

1060
01:01:19,569 --> 01:01:22,037
Peace isn't a political goal.

1061
01:01:22,071 --> 01:01:23,640
It's a human goal.

1062
01:01:26,849 --> 01:01:29,347
Just being around me puts you at risk, Patricia.

1063
01:01:29,381 --> 01:01:31,303
I know.

1064
01:01:32,685 --> 01:01:36,112
I'm not going anywhere.

1065
01:01:39,322 --> 01:01:40,763
Marry me.

1066
01:01:58,465 --> 01:02:01,669
Albert Spear, Hitler's chief engineer and architect

1067
01:02:01,703 --> 01:02:03,465
manufactured the munitions.

1068
01:02:03,498 --> 01:02:07,055
He was the only Nazi tried at Nuremberg to admit his guilt

1069
01:02:07,089 --> 01:02:08,338
for his complicity

1070
01:02:08,372 --> 01:02:10,839
for taking part in the creation of a...

1071
01:02:10,873 --> 01:02:13,212
depraved and evil apparatus.

1072
01:02:13,246 --> 01:02:16,322
He acknowledged he was just as guilty

1073
01:02:16,356 --> 01:02:20,522
as the men who used the system to commit genocide.

1074
01:02:20,557 --> 01:02:25,012
I knew firsthand how we got into this war.

1075
01:02:25,045 --> 01:02:29,853
I knew firsthand our prospects were hopeless

1076
01:02:29,887 --> 01:02:33,636
and I knew it was not going to end.

1077
01:02:50,825 --> 01:02:53,036
New York Times, Washington Desk.

1078
01:02:53,070 --> 01:02:54,992
Uh, Neil Sheehan.

1079
01:02:58,168 --> 01:02:59,641
Sheehan.

1080
01:03:00,958 --> 01:03:02,495
Hello?

1081
01:03:03,715 --> 01:03:06,214
Who is this?

1082
01:03:09,039 --> 01:03:10,383
This is Daniel Ellsberg.

1083
01:03:10,417 --> 01:03:11,505
A few years ago you said

1084
01:03:11,539 --> 01:03:13,236
if I ever had a good story, you'd write it.

1085
01:03:13,271 --> 01:03:14,584
Yeah, I remember.

1086
01:03:17,119 --> 01:03:18,720
I've got one.

1087
01:03:20,453 --> 01:03:22,984
What do you know about the Pentagon's war study?

1088
01:03:23,018 --> 01:03:25,294
Nothing. No one's seen it.

1089
01:03:25,327 --> 01:03:27,570
That could change.

1090
01:03:41,231 --> 01:03:42,736
You realize I can't make any commitment

1091
01:03:42,770 --> 01:03:44,404
that we'll publish this until I've read it

1092
01:03:44,438 --> 01:03:45,462
and my editors have read...

1093
01:03:45,496 --> 01:03:46,424
How long will that take?

1094
01:03:46,458 --> 01:03:47,707
There's no telling.

1095
01:03:47,740 --> 01:03:49,309
We'll have to confirm its authenticity...

1096
01:03:49,344 --> 01:03:50,336
They're authentic.

1097
01:03:50,369 --> 01:03:52,516
...merits, and there still could be some resistance.

1098
01:03:52,550 --> 01:03:54,152
Resistance? On what grounds?

1099
01:03:54,185 --> 01:03:55,914
I mean, this incomprehensible.

1100
01:03:55,949 --> 01:03:57,614
I mean, this is a huge story.

1101
01:03:57,648 --> 01:03:59,538
These are classified internal documents

1102
01:03:59,572 --> 01:04:00,563
leaked in a time of war.

1103
01:04:00,598 --> 01:04:03,001
An undeclared, endless, sickening killing war.

1104
01:04:03,035 --> 01:04:04,283
That is not the point.

1105
01:04:04,317 --> 01:04:05,534
That is the point.

1106
01:04:17,529 --> 01:04:18,809
Look, I'm just warning you

1107
01:04:18,842 --> 01:04:20,477
there are bound to be some debate

1108
01:04:20,510 --> 01:04:23,363
over the legality and wisdom of going to press with this.

1109
01:04:23,396 --> 01:04:24,548
Good, there should be debate--

1110
01:04:24,582 --> 01:04:26,729
about the legality and the wisdom of what it says in them.

1111
01:04:29,264 --> 01:04:31,603
Complete, the study is 47 volumes.

1112
01:04:31,637 --> 01:04:32,725
There's 43 upstairs.

1113
01:04:32,759 --> 01:04:34,039
I've held back anything

1114
01:04:34,074 --> 01:04:35,835
that includes secret codification

1115
01:04:35,870 --> 01:04:37,983
troop positions, and any other material

1116
01:04:38,017 --> 01:04:40,325
that I feel compromises national security.

1117
01:04:40,358 --> 01:04:42,153
You mind if I ask you a question?

1118
01:04:42,186 --> 01:04:44,973
What do you want out of all this?

1119
01:04:46,322 --> 01:04:49,014
I want to get in the way of the bombing and killing.

1120
01:05:38,683 --> 01:05:40,509
Patricia?

1121
01:05:40,543 --> 01:05:41,824
I'm here.

1122
01:05:44,327 --> 01:05:46,474
Nixon danced last night at Tricia's wedding.

1123
01:05:46,507 --> 01:05:48,300
Gosh, that's a scary thought.

1124
01:05:48,335 --> 01:05:49,969
Here's another funny story

1125
01:05:50,002 --> 01:05:52,213
you might be interested in.

1126
01:05:54,428 --> 01:05:56,060
Oh, my gosh...

1127
01:05:57,441 --> 01:05:58,850
I don't believe it.

1128
01:06:02,380 --> 01:06:03,756
There's no going back now.

1129
01:06:03,790 --> 01:06:06,642
Well, somebody had to tell the truth.

1130
01:06:18,380 --> 01:06:20,623
Jesus Christ!

1131
01:06:20,656 --> 01:06:22,643
...news was overshadowed

1132
01:06:22,676 --> 01:06:24,822
by the appearance in today's New York Times

1133
01:06:24,856 --> 01:06:26,297
of an apparently covert study

1134
01:06:26,332 --> 01:06:28,158
outlining our involvement in Vietnam.

1135
01:06:28,191 --> 01:06:30,082
No reaction as of yet from the White House...

1136
01:06:30,115 --> 01:06:31,204
All right!

1137
01:06:31,238 --> 01:06:34,057
That's the way to do it!

1138
01:06:36,079 --> 01:06:38,899
The New York Times is finished at the White House.

1139
01:06:38,933 --> 01:06:41,079
We can't have any more of these goddamn leaks.

1140
01:06:41,113 --> 01:06:42,298
Some kind of special unit

1141
01:06:42,332 --> 01:06:44,028
should be put together to see to it.

1142
01:06:44,063 --> 01:06:45,312
Plumbers.

1143
01:06:45,346 --> 01:06:46,979
To fix the leaks.

1144
01:06:47,013 --> 01:06:49,608
Where does the president want us to go from here?

1145
01:06:49,642 --> 01:06:51,950
The Times is preparing the next installment

1146
01:06:51,983 --> 01:06:53,104
as we speak.

1147
01:06:53,137 --> 01:06:55,059
And we cannot allow that to happen.

1148
01:06:55,093 --> 01:06:58,010
I can ask the Times to voluntarily cease and desist

1149
01:06:58,043 --> 01:06:59,484
from publication.

1150
01:06:59,518 --> 01:07:02,947
If they refuse, we'll take the gloves off.

1151
01:07:02,982 --> 01:07:05,576
Justice can enjoin them from publishing further.

1152
01:07:05,611 --> 01:07:06,859
Stop the presses?

1153
01:07:06,893 --> 01:07:09,103
Is that done? Has that ever been done?

1154
01:07:10,965 --> 01:07:12,246
No.

1155
01:07:15,519 --> 01:07:17,441
Set it in motion.

1156
01:07:18,693 --> 01:07:20,936
Where did the Times get the Pentagon study?

1157
01:07:20,969 --> 01:07:22,699
The FBI has a suspect.

1158
01:07:22,734 --> 01:07:24,238
Daniel Ellsberg.

1159
01:07:24,272 --> 01:07:25,232
Who is he?

1160
01:07:25,266 --> 01:07:26,258
They're working on it.

1161
01:07:26,292 --> 01:07:27,861
Well, find out and neutralize him.

1162
01:07:29,241 --> 01:07:30,459
Take right.

1163
01:07:30,493 --> 01:07:31,966
Got it.

1164
01:07:33,089 --> 01:07:34,049
Bedroom's clear.

1165
01:07:35,367 --> 01:07:36,648
Kitchen's clear.

1166
01:07:38,605 --> 01:07:40,302
Clear.

1167
01:07:40,337 --> 01:07:41,297
Ellsberg's gone.

1168
01:07:41,330 --> 01:07:42,483
The place is empty.

1169
01:07:46,589 --> 01:07:47,774
Here's the last one.

1170
01:07:59,222 --> 01:08:01,786
You know, you guys are the first people ever to knock.

1171
01:08:01,819 --> 01:08:03,421
May we come in, Mr. Russo?

1172
01:08:03,455 --> 01:08:06,082
No, no, no. I'll come out.

1173
01:08:07,719 --> 01:08:11,693
We want to talk to you about Daniel Ellsberg.

1174
01:08:11,727 --> 01:08:14,740
Well, I don't wish to talk to you.

1175
01:08:14,774 --> 01:08:16,696
Do you have a lawyer?

1176
01:08:17,820 --> 01:08:20,480
You better get one.

1177
01:08:26,894 --> 01:08:28,463
Carol Ellsberg?

1178
01:08:40,329 --> 01:08:42,989
Did you know that your Top Secret Control Officer

1179
01:08:43,023 --> 01:08:44,239
was sleeping with him?

1180
01:08:44,272 --> 01:08:45,714
That's none of my business.

1181
01:08:45,748 --> 01:08:48,536
It is if she was involved in copy and distribution.

1182
01:08:48,570 --> 01:08:50,908
She had absolutely nothing to do with this.

1183
01:08:50,943 --> 01:08:53,185
And what did you have to do with it?

1184
01:08:53,219 --> 01:08:54,436
Nothing whatsoever.

1185
01:08:54,470 --> 01:08:56,263
Excuse me.

1186
01:09:01,107 --> 01:09:03,895
The Times declined our request to cease and desist.

1187
01:09:03,929 --> 01:09:06,524
Put a muzzle on the sons of bitches.

1188
01:09:06,557 --> 01:09:08,191
What about Ellsberg?

1189
01:09:08,225 --> 01:09:10,821
Kissinger thinks he's brilliant, but unbalanced.

1190
01:09:10,855 --> 01:09:12,520
It's obvious that he's flipped.

1191
01:09:12,554 --> 01:09:13,771
From hawk to peacenik.

1192
01:09:13,805 --> 01:09:15,374
They think it could be drugs.

1193
01:09:15,408 --> 01:09:16,529
Is he alone in this?

1194
01:09:16,562 --> 01:09:18,965
He married a very rich girl, Patricia Marx--

1195
01:09:18,999 --> 01:09:19,927
Marx Toy Company.

1196
01:09:19,961 --> 01:09:21,306
Hoover knows her father.

1197
01:09:21,340 --> 01:09:23,582
Christ! No wonder the FBI can't find him.

1198
01:09:23,616 --> 01:09:25,186
They're probably not looking.

1199
01:09:25,220 --> 01:09:27,238
Well, they say he's gone underground.

1200
01:09:27,272 --> 01:09:29,258
Could he be talking to the Russians?

1201
01:09:29,291 --> 01:09:31,278
Chinese?

1202
01:09:31,311 --> 01:09:32,689
How much does he know?

1203
01:09:32,723 --> 01:09:35,253
How badly can the son of a bitch hurt us?

1204
01:09:37,116 --> 01:09:38,941
We should've never have gone to press

1205
01:09:38,975 --> 01:09:40,320
with this in the first place.

1206
01:09:40,354 --> 01:09:42,693
For crying out loud, it's the First Amendment

1207
01:09:42,727 --> 01:09:44,585
to the Constitution of the United States!

1208
01:09:49,076 --> 01:09:50,004
Hello?

1209
01:09:50,038 --> 01:09:51,030
Dan, it's Sheehan. Yeah.

1210
01:09:51,064 --> 01:09:52,986
We're pulling the next segment on the papers.

1211
01:09:53,020 --> 01:09:54,172
What are you talking about?

1212
01:09:54,207 --> 01:09:55,872
It's coming directly from the Oval Office.

1213
01:09:55,906 --> 01:09:57,346
They're not going to arrest the entire staff

1214
01:09:57,380 --> 01:09:58,886
of The New York Times. Just keep printing it.

1215
01:09:58,920 --> 01:10:00,008
You're not listening to me, Dan.

1216
01:10:00,042 --> 01:10:01,066
They stopped the presses.

1217
01:10:01,100 --> 01:10:03,086
Well, they did. That's illegal. They can't do that.

1218
01:10:03,120 --> 01:10:04,529
We've been enjoined for prior restraint.

1219
01:10:04,563 --> 01:10:05,491
We've pulled the story.

1220
01:10:05,525 --> 01:10:07,255
Nixon had Mitchell claim Print it anyway!

1221
01:10:07,288 --> 01:10:09,275
that publication is a grave threat to national security.

1222
01:10:09,308 --> 01:10:10,845
Bullshit! Nixon and Mitchell are a goddamn threat

1223
01:10:10,879 --> 01:10:11,904
to national security.

1224
01:10:11,937 --> 01:10:13,731
Don't tell me that! There's nothing we can do!

1225
01:10:13,765 --> 01:10:15,399
We can't do it! You can print the story!

1226
01:10:15,433 --> 01:10:16,008
It's over, Dan!

1227
01:10:16,042 --> 01:10:16,842
It's not over!

1228
01:10:16,876 --> 01:10:17,836
You'll have to find another way

1229
01:10:17,870 --> 01:10:18,798
to get them out.

1230
01:10:18,832 --> 01:10:19,952
Sheehan!

1231
01:10:19,986 --> 01:10:20,914
Wait...

1232
01:10:20,948 --> 01:10:22,005
Shit!

1233
01:10:26,591 --> 01:10:27,872
Shit.

1234
01:10:33,966 --> 01:10:35,375
What are you doing?

1235
01:10:35,409 --> 01:10:37,747
Excuse me! What are you doing?!

1236
01:10:38,147 --> 01:10:44,347
[The 1971 rupture and this 2003 film are two antibody tellings of the same lie.]

1237
01:10:44,947 --> 01:10:51,147
[Hanyok’s SIGINT pack (2005–06) is the third. The film is the bridge title.]

1238
01:10:57,950 --> 01:10:59,648
...allied forces in Vietnam...

1239
01:10:59,682 --> 01:11:02,246
Dan, you've been on every news channel.

1240
01:11:02,279 --> 01:11:05,067
They're calling them the "Pentagon Papers"

1241
01:11:05,101 --> 01:11:08,369
and the FBI has named you as the probable source.

1242
01:11:08,403 --> 01:11:10,102
The manhunt's nationwide.

1243
01:11:10,135 --> 01:11:12,249
They know everything about you.

1244
01:11:12,283 --> 01:11:13,628
They know what school you went to

1245
01:11:13,662 --> 01:11:15,199
how long you were there, your family.

1246
01:11:15,233 --> 01:11:16,354
They know about me...

1247
01:11:16,387 --> 01:11:18,534
Nixon got a court order enjoining The New York Times

1248
01:11:18,568 --> 01:11:19,496
to cease publication.

1249
01:11:19,530 --> 01:11:20,458
He did what?

1250
01:11:20,492 --> 01:11:21,452
The Times pulled the story.

1251
01:11:21,486 --> 01:11:22,927
I called The Washington Post.

1252
01:11:22,960 --> 01:11:24,594
They're flying up to get a copy.

1253
01:11:24,629 --> 01:11:26,198
Will they be able to go to press

1254
01:11:26,232 --> 01:11:28,218
after what's happened to the Times?

1255
01:11:28,251 --> 01:11:29,628
I don't know.

1256
01:11:29,662 --> 01:11:31,008
What if they don't?

1257
01:11:31,041 --> 01:11:31,970
I don't know.

1258
01:11:32,003 --> 01:11:33,475
I mean, how can they?

1259
01:11:33,510 --> 01:11:35,592
If Nixon can stop the Times...

1260
01:11:35,626 --> 01:11:39,632
I don't know, Patricia! I don't know!

1261
01:11:39,667 --> 01:11:41,973
...with Saigon the main objective.

1262
01:11:44,604 --> 01:11:46,462
We're not just going to give up.

1263
01:11:46,497 --> 01:11:47,873
...a few major battles.

1264
01:11:47,906 --> 01:11:50,727
The third of six days of action has been heavy.

1265
01:11:50,760 --> 01:11:52,875
The allies estimate they have killed

1266
01:11:52,909 --> 01:11:55,536
more than 6,000 enemy troops in that period.

1267
01:11:55,570 --> 01:11:58,294
American casualties are put at 360 dead

1268
01:11:58,328 --> 01:12:00,378
and more than 1,000 wounded.

1269
01:12:00,412 --> 01:12:03,199
I didn't say anything about giving up.

1270
01:12:03,234 --> 01:12:05,957
...South Vietnamese have been killed.

1271
01:12:05,992 --> 01:12:07,914
Good evening. Publication of parts

1272
01:12:07,947 --> 01:12:09,997
of the 47-volume, top secret history

1273
01:12:10,031 --> 01:12:11,953
of American involvement in Vietnam

1274
01:12:11,987 --> 01:12:14,807
has triggered a major constitutional legal battle

1275
01:12:14,841 --> 01:12:17,661
over government secrecy and freedom of the press.

1276
01:12:17,695 --> 01:12:20,419
The man most frequently suggested as the source

1277
01:12:20,453 --> 01:12:23,272
for the Pentagon documents is Daniel Ellsberg, 40

1278
01:12:23,307 --> 01:12:26,191
PhD with honors, one-time Marine, now a researcher

1279
01:12:26,224 --> 01:12:28,562
at Massachusetts Institute of Technology.

1280
01:12:28,597 --> 01:12:31,610
Ellsberg was a Pentagon and State Department analyst

1281
01:12:31,643 --> 01:12:33,212
during the Vietnam buildup.

1282
01:12:33,246 --> 01:12:35,585
Later, he worked on the now-controversial

1283
01:12:35,619 --> 01:12:37,156
Pentagon study of the war.

1284
01:12:37,190 --> 01:12:40,299
Perhaps because of his clearly delicate legal position

1285
01:12:40,332 --> 01:12:42,799
he will not talk right now of whatever part

1286
01:12:42,833 --> 01:12:45,589
he played in the release of the secret documents

1287
01:12:45,623 --> 01:12:46,743
but he talks freely

1288
01:12:46,778 --> 01:12:49,405
of what he hopes their release will accomplish

1289
01:12:49,439 --> 01:12:51,553
and what turned him from hawk to dove

1290
01:12:51,587 --> 01:12:54,663
such as what he believes was official coldheartedness

1291
01:12:54,698 --> 01:12:56,973
towards civilian casualties in the war.

1292
01:12:57,006 --> 01:13:00,852
Fact is, in the seven to 10,000 pages of this study

1293
01:13:00,886 --> 01:13:03,738
I don't think there is a line in them

1294
01:13:03,772 --> 01:13:07,745
that contains an estimate of the likely impact

1295
01:13:07,780 --> 01:13:10,407
of our policy on the overall causalities

1296
01:13:10,442 --> 01:13:13,646
among the Vietnamese, the refugees to be caused...

1297
01:13:13,679 --> 01:13:15,763
I'll get some men over to CBS right away.

1298
01:13:15,796 --> 01:13:17,301
It won't do any good.

1299
01:13:17,335 --> 01:13:19,321
They're doing the interview at some secret location.

1300
01:13:19,355 --> 01:13:22,015
My father had a favorite line in the Bible

1301
01:13:22,048 --> 01:13:24,740
which I used to hear a great deal when I was a kid.

1302
01:13:24,775 --> 01:13:27,113
"The truth shall make you free."

1303
01:13:27,146 --> 01:13:29,646
And I hope that the truth that's out now

1304
01:13:29,680 --> 01:13:34,232
that's out of the safes-- and there is no way, no way

1305
01:13:34,265 --> 01:13:36,059
to get it back into the safes--

1306
01:13:36,093 --> 01:13:38,399
I hope that truth will free us of this war.

1307
01:13:38,433 --> 01:13:41,798
I hope that we will put this war behind us

1308
01:13:41,833 --> 01:13:43,466
and we'll learn from it in such a way

1309
01:13:43,499 --> 01:13:46,383
that the history of the next 20 years

1310
01:13:46,418 --> 01:13:49,974
will read nothing like the history of the last 20 years.

1311
01:14:03,637 --> 01:14:05,558
The Appellate Court stepped aside.

1312
01:14:05,592 --> 01:14:08,830
Times and the Post vs. The United States

1313
01:14:08,863 --> 01:14:11,650
have been bumped up to the Supreme Court.

1314
01:14:11,685 --> 01:14:13,703
They start in the morning.

1315
01:14:16,205 --> 01:14:18,865
We've done all we can.

1316
01:14:24,094 --> 01:14:25,855
I think it's time for me to go in.

1317
01:14:28,359 --> 01:14:31,659
I wasn't ready to hear you say that.

1318
01:14:33,680 --> 01:14:35,186
We just heard that Senator Gravel

1319
01:14:35,220 --> 01:14:37,334
of Alaska was prevented from reading the paper

1320
01:14:37,368 --> 01:14:38,553
on the Senate floor.

1321
01:14:38,586 --> 01:14:42,048
It's up to the Supreme Court, the First Amendment issue.

1322
01:14:42,081 --> 01:14:44,389
Dan, let's talk about your case.

1323
01:14:44,423 --> 01:14:46,922
So, where do we stand?

1324
01:14:46,955 --> 01:14:48,493
Charges against you will include

1325
01:14:48,527 --> 01:14:49,904
stealing government property

1326
01:14:49,937 --> 01:14:51,828
receiving, communicating and retaining

1327
01:14:51,861 --> 01:14:53,271
national defense documents.

1328
01:14:53,305 --> 01:14:56,285
Conspiracy, multiple violations of the Espionage Act.

1329
01:14:56,319 --> 01:14:57,535
Possibly even treason.

1330
01:14:57,568 --> 01:14:59,266
We really can't tell how far they'll go.

1331
01:14:59,301 --> 01:15:02,505
Sounds like I'm in for a slap on the wrist.

1332
01:15:02,539 --> 01:15:04,782
What's the worst that could happen?

1333
01:15:04,816 --> 01:15:06,449
There's no reason to sugarcoat this.

1334
01:15:06,482 --> 01:15:08,052
A lot of people are extremely grateful to you

1335
01:15:08,086 --> 01:15:09,078
for what you've done

1336
01:15:09,112 --> 01:15:11,066
but Dick Nixon is not one of them.

1337
01:15:11,100 --> 01:15:12,670
Apparently, the more he thinks about it

1338
01:15:12,704 --> 01:15:14,113
the more outraged... Um...

1339
01:15:14,146 --> 01:15:16,197
Short of the death penalty

1340
01:15:16,231 --> 01:15:18,153
life in prison is probably what they'll shoot for.

1341
01:15:22,772 --> 01:15:24,565
The sooner you turn yourself in

1342
01:15:24,599 --> 01:15:28,670
the sooner we can start fighting.

1343
01:15:28,704 --> 01:15:31,556
Let's arrange for my surrender.

1344
01:15:44,768 --> 01:15:47,043
Step back, please.

1345
01:15:48,776 --> 01:15:50,473
Dr. Ellsberg, comment on the indictment

1346
01:15:50,508 --> 01:15:53,039
the Attorney General read against you this morning.

1347
01:15:53,073 --> 01:15:54,385
Eleven counts.

1348
01:15:54,419 --> 01:15:56,181
You could get more than 100 years.

1349
01:15:56,215 --> 01:15:59,130
I don't regret releasing the papers

1350
01:15:59,165 --> 01:16:01,600
only that so many lives might have been saved

1351
01:16:01,634 --> 01:16:03,139
if I'd only done it sooner.

1352
01:16:03,173 --> 01:16:04,711
Dr. Ellsberg, one more comment.

1353
01:16:04,745 --> 01:16:05,832
One more comment!

1354
01:16:26,741 --> 01:16:28,117
You have the right to remain silent

1355
01:16:28,151 --> 01:16:29,304
and refuse to answer questions.

1356
01:16:29,338 --> 01:16:30,266
Do you understand?

1357
01:16:30,300 --> 01:16:31,932
Yes.

1358
01:16:31,967 --> 01:16:33,921
Any thing you say may be used against you

1359
01:16:33,955 --> 01:16:35,525
in a court of law, you understand?

1360
01:16:35,558 --> 01:16:37,448
Yes.

1361
01:16:37,482 --> 01:16:39,147
You have the right to consult an attorney

1362
01:16:39,182 --> 01:16:40,430
before speaking with the police

1363
01:16:40,464 --> 01:16:42,707
and to have an attorney present now and in the future.

1364
01:16:42,742 --> 01:16:43,765
You understand all that?

1365
01:16:43,800 --> 01:16:45,144
Yes.

1366
01:16:48,673 --> 01:16:49,858
Both sides seemed to score points

1367
01:16:49,891 --> 01:16:51,460
with the justices in oral arguments today.

1368
01:16:51,495 --> 01:16:53,449
The government stressed the sensitive nature

1369
01:16:53,482 --> 01:16:54,763
of the Pentagon Papers

1370
01:16:54,797 --> 01:16:57,617
and the risk to our troops abroad by their publication.

1371
01:16:57,651 --> 01:17:00,278
Lawyers for The New York Times and Washington Post

1372
01:17:00,312 --> 01:17:02,940
countered that the papers are a historical record

1373
01:17:02,974 --> 01:17:05,729
which poses no danger to soldiers overseas

1374
01:17:05,763 --> 01:17:08,390
and which the public here at home has a right to read.

1375
01:17:08,425 --> 01:17:10,539
The case was submitted late today.

1376
01:17:10,572 --> 01:17:13,681
A ruling could be handed down at any time.

1377
01:17:26,284 --> 01:17:27,854
Yeah?

1378
01:17:27,887 --> 01:17:29,649
I'm on my way.

1379
01:17:29,683 --> 01:17:31,285
Decision's in.

1380
01:17:43,150 --> 01:17:45,874
The Court has reached a decision.

1381
01:17:45,907 --> 01:17:48,471
The Majority Opinion will now be distributed.

1382
01:17:49,000 --> 01:17:55,200
[L1: the Court lets the presses run. The 2003 film retells that win for a post-9/11 living room.]

1383
01:17:57,965 --> 01:17:58,957
Dan...

1384
01:17:58,990 --> 01:18:00,238
What happened?

1385
01:18:00,273 --> 01:18:01,715
The Supreme Court--

1386
01:18:01,748 --> 01:18:04,278
they ruled six to three

1387
01:18:04,313 --> 01:18:07,325
and the papers are going to press as we speak.

1388
01:18:11,720 --> 01:18:14,091
And we've posted bail.

1389
01:18:14,126 --> 01:18:16,303
So we're going to be able to put all this behind us

1390
01:18:16,338 --> 01:18:18,452
and with the publication of the papers

1391
01:18:18,485 --> 01:18:19,991
they're now legal, so...

1392
01:18:20,025 --> 01:18:22,427
I mean, what law have you broken, Dan?

1393
01:18:29,067 --> 01:18:31,598
This has nothing to do with my case.

1394
01:18:31,633 --> 01:18:34,131
They're still going to try me for treason.

1395
01:18:34,165 --> 01:18:36,856
Let me see the decision.

1396
01:18:36,890 --> 01:18:39,134
It's Justice Black's opinion.

1397
01:18:43,432 --> 01:18:44,552
"In the First Amendment

1398
01:18:44,586 --> 01:18:46,348
"the Founding Fathers gave the press

1399
01:18:46,382 --> 01:18:47,855
"a protection they must have

1400
01:18:47,889 --> 01:18:49,843
"to fulfill its role in our democracy

1401
01:18:49,877 --> 01:18:52,472
"and paramount among the duties of the free press

1402
01:18:52,506 --> 01:18:53,851
"is the duty to prevent

1403
01:18:53,885 --> 01:18:56,416
"any part of the government from deceiving the people

1404
01:18:56,451 --> 01:18:58,276
"and sending them off to distant lands

1405
01:18:58,309 --> 01:19:03,438
"to die of foreign fevers and foreign shot and shell.

1406
01:19:03,473 --> 01:19:04,625
"Far from deserving condemnation

1407
01:19:04,658 --> 01:19:06,132
"The New York Times and Washington Post

1408
01:19:06,166 --> 01:19:09,114
"nobly did precisely that which the Founders hoped

1409
01:19:09,148 --> 01:19:11,166
"and trusted they would do.

1410
01:19:11,200 --> 01:19:14,885
"The press must be left free to publish news

1411
01:19:14,920 --> 01:19:18,893
whatever the source without censorship."

1412
01:19:25,083 --> 01:19:29,154
I don't think I'm going to be back here again.

1413
01:19:29,187 --> 01:19:30,501
Thank you.

1414
01:19:30,535 --> 01:19:33,162
You're welcome, Daniel.

1415
01:20:47,040 --> 01:20:50,084
Vamos. Abiento.

1416
01:20:58,135 --> 01:21:00,442
So Ellsberg's a national hero

1417
01:21:00,475 --> 01:21:03,295
and The New York Times will probably win a damn Pulitzer--

1418
01:21:03,329 --> 01:21:05,604
all for stealing documents.

1419
01:21:05,639 --> 01:21:07,239
These people are thieves.

1420
01:21:07,273 --> 01:21:08,939
Apparently Ellsberg had an accomplice.

1421
01:21:08,972 --> 01:21:10,285
We're going after him as well.

1422
01:21:10,320 --> 01:21:14,069
Believe me, neither will see the light of day again.

1423
01:21:14,103 --> 01:21:15,223
That's fine about their trials

1424
01:21:15,258 --> 01:21:17,051
but the President still wants to get the dirt.

1425
01:21:17,086 --> 01:21:19,232
He wants the son of a bitch destroyed.

1426
01:21:19,265 --> 01:21:20,899
Morning, Mr. President.

1427
01:21:20,934 --> 01:21:22,278
Morning, Mr. President.

1428
01:21:22,312 --> 01:21:24,234
Mr. President.

1429
01:21:24,268 --> 01:21:27,504
Our little operation out in Beverly Hills went smoothly.

1430
01:21:27,538 --> 01:21:30,967
Here's what the Plumbers found in Dr. Fielding's office.

1431
01:21:45,302 --> 01:21:47,353
Not that it didn't go well in there today

1432
01:21:47,386 --> 01:21:49,853
and not that I think our trial is going badly in general

1433
01:21:49,888 --> 01:21:50,944
but which countries

1434
01:21:50,978 --> 01:21:53,060
have non-extradition policies with the US?

1435
01:21:53,094 --> 01:21:54,567
...special news bulletin.

1436
01:21:54,601 --> 01:21:58,800
Five men were arrested at 2:30 this morning

1437
01:21:58,833 --> 01:22:00,145
while they were in the process

1438
01:22:00,180 --> 01:22:01,910
of wiretapping and bugging the offices

1439
01:22:01,943 --> 01:22:04,474
of the Democratic National Committee at Washington's

1440
01:22:04,509 --> 01:22:07,136
Watergate hotel and apartment complex.

1441
01:22:07,170 --> 01:22:10,824
The identities of the burglars remain unanswered at this hour.

1442
01:22:20,188 --> 01:22:21,726
What is it?

1443
01:22:21,760 --> 01:22:24,001
I couldn't sleep.

1444
01:22:24,036 --> 01:22:26,021
Yeah?

1445
01:22:26,056 --> 01:22:28,459
You know, it hit me today

1446
01:22:28,493 --> 01:22:32,564
that it's been over a year with your case.

1447
01:22:32,597 --> 01:22:35,770
Sometimes it feels like it's just...

1448
01:22:35,803 --> 01:22:38,559
never going to end.

1449
01:22:40,838 --> 01:22:42,023
Everyone always thought

1450
01:22:42,056 --> 01:22:44,684
I was destined to do something great with my life.

1451
01:22:44,718 --> 01:22:46,607
Make a mark.

1452
01:22:46,642 --> 01:22:49,430
When my mother and sister died...

1453
01:22:51,098 --> 01:22:53,020
My fa...

1454
01:22:53,054 --> 01:22:56,227
I only thought of myself.

1455
01:22:56,261 --> 01:22:59,402
And when my illusion of America died

1456
01:22:59,436 --> 01:23:02,351
I did the same thing.

1457
01:23:02,386 --> 01:23:08,572
I wanted to bring the government and the nation down.

1458
01:23:12,069 --> 01:23:16,588
I loved them, and I was angry, and they disappointed me.

1459
01:23:21,176 --> 01:23:23,514
I'm going to be remembered as a traitor.

1460
01:23:23,548 --> 01:23:26,784
My children will be ashamed

1461
01:23:26,819 --> 01:23:30,440
their name disgraced.

1462
01:23:48,045 --> 01:23:49,935
All rise.

1463
01:23:49,968 --> 01:23:53,751
The Honorable Judge William Matthew Byrne presiding.

1464
01:23:53,785 --> 01:23:55,001
Be seated.

1465
01:24:02,154 --> 01:24:05,358
Commencing on April 26th, the government has made

1466
01:24:05,392 --> 01:24:07,506
an extraordinary series of disclosures

1467
01:24:07,541 --> 01:24:10,264
regarding the conduct of several governmental agencies

1468
01:24:10,298 --> 01:24:12,861
regarding the defendants in this case.

1469
01:24:12,894 --> 01:24:14,273
It is my responsibility

1470
01:24:14,306 --> 01:24:16,485
to assess the effect of this conduct

1471
01:24:16,518 --> 01:24:18,472
upon the rights of the defendants

1472
01:24:18,507 --> 01:24:20,942
and their opportunities for a fair trial

1473
01:24:20,975 --> 01:24:24,244
with due process of law.

1474
01:24:24,278 --> 01:24:25,624
Along with the country at large

1475
01:24:25,657 --> 01:24:28,124
this court has recently become aware of a special unit

1476
01:24:28,158 --> 01:24:30,368
that was established, reporting to the White House

1477
01:24:30,403 --> 01:24:32,260
which undertook both the burglary

1478
01:24:32,294 --> 01:24:34,281
at the office of Dr. Lewis Fielding

1479
01:24:34,315 --> 01:24:37,295
and the break-in at the Watergate Hotel.

1480
01:24:43,004 --> 01:24:46,080
The totality of the circumstances of this case

1481
01:24:46,114 --> 01:24:47,972
offends a sense of justice.

1482
01:24:48,006 --> 01:24:52,429
The bizarre events have incurably infected

1483
01:24:52,463 --> 01:24:54,416
the prosecution of this case.

1484
01:24:54,451 --> 01:24:58,072
Therefore, I have decided to declare a mistrial and grant...

1485
01:24:59,709 --> 01:25:02,145
And grant the motion to dismiss.

1486
01:25:11,413 --> 01:25:13,655
Thank God for Richard Nixon.

1487
01:25:14,200 --> 01:25:20,400
[Prosecution collapsed after White House crimes against Ellsberg — including the Fielding break-in.]

1488
01:25:21,000 --> 01:25:27,200
[This movie was not allowed a theatrical life. FX was the ceiling.]

1489
01:25:27,800 --> 01:25:34,000
[No HD remaster followed. Twenty-plus years later the best source is still a PAL DVD.]

1490
01:25:34,600 --> 01:25:40,800
[That is the same containment: public enough to exist, not public enough to become a ritual.]

1491
01:25:41,400 --> 01:25:45,692
[See Paradigm Threat: AU-058 (2001–2005 offensive) · AU-059 (McNaughton) · AU-060 (RAND).]

1492
01:25:46,492 --> 01:25:50,818
For me as an American, democracy is sacred

1493
01:25:50,853 --> 01:25:52,647
something worth fighting for.

1494
01:25:52,680 --> 01:25:57,263
And if my own greatest act of patriotism

1495
01:25:57,298 --> 01:26:00,374
was treason, so be it.

1496
01:26:01,000 --> 01:26:07,200
[By ~2006 the antibody lane bunkers. Tonkin still rarely reaches a history class.]

1497
01:26:07,800 --> 01:26:14,000
[The 1996–2000 NSA film window (AU-062) pre-seeds this crest: Lebowski / Mummy open / Mission to Mars.]

1498
01:26:19,005 --> 01:26:20,351
I think the lesson is

1499
01:26:20,384 --> 01:26:23,108
that the people of this country, um, can't afford

1500
01:26:23,142 --> 01:26:25,769
to let the President run the country by himself

1501
01:26:25,803 --> 01:26:28,751
even foreign affairs, any more than domestic affairs

1502
01:26:28,785 --> 01:26:30,131
without the help of the Congress

1503
01:26:30,164 --> 01:26:31,540
without the help of the public.

1504
01:26:40,521 --> 01:26:44,045
The system shouldn't change, I think, to begin with.

1505
01:26:44,080 --> 01:26:47,445
Very hard, really, the way our system operates now

1506
01:26:47,479 --> 01:26:50,875
for a truly frank, honest man

1507
01:26:50,910 --> 01:26:53,569
to stay in that system indefinitely

1508
01:26:53,602 --> 01:26:56,744
without being weeded out or fired or made apathetic

1509
01:26:56,777 --> 01:26:59,244
or in effect, corrupted in the end.

1510
01:27:06,557 --> 01:27:07,837
Good evening.

1511
01:27:07,872 --> 01:27:10,243
A quarter century of American involvement

1512
01:27:10,277 --> 01:27:11,781
in South Vietnam is over.

1513
01:27:11,816 --> 01:27:13,514
At mid-afternoon, Saigon time

1514
01:27:13,547 --> 01:27:15,789
and armada of 81 US Marine helicopters

1515
01:27:15,824 --> 01:27:17,457
descended on the South Vietnamese capital.

1516
01:27:17,491 --> 01:27:21,529
More than 6,000 persons were evacuated

1517
01:27:21,563 --> 01:27:22,844
plucked from the US Embassy grounds

1518
01:27:22,879 --> 01:27:25,089
from rooftops throughout the city

1519
01:27:25,122 --> 01:27:26,885
and from the nearby Tan Son Nhut Airport.

1520
01:27:26,918 --> 01:27:28,616
It was a chaotic scene in Saigon today

1521
01:27:28,650 --> 01:27:31,084
when those US Marines arrived.

1522
01:27:31,118 --> 01:27:33,650
Hundreds, perhaps thousands, of South Vietnamese

1523
01:27:33,684 --> 01:27:35,574
fought to get inside the American Embassy compound

1524
01:27:35,608 --> 01:27:38,684
to get on those American helicopters leaving Saigon.

1525
01:27:38,717 --> 01:27:42,051
Some Vietnamese tried to scale the ten-foot Embassy walls

1526
01:27:42,085 --> 01:27:44,039
and got caught on the barbed wire.

1527
01:27:44,073 --> 01:27:46,124
The Vietnamese cried and shouted

1528
01:27:46,157 --> 01:27:48,785
"We want to go, too," and during all this

1529
01:27:48,818 --> 01:27:49,971
inside the American Embassy

1530
01:27:50,005 --> 01:27:52,857
officials were burning the last of their documents.

1531
01:27:52,891 --> 01:27:54,588
Dan Rather, CBS News, New York.

1532
01:27:54,622 --> 01:27:56,480
Thank you for joining us.
